# REPAIR BRIEF r1 — Book 8, Movement 4 "Inside Walls" (ch20–26)

Same author, same method (NO-SOURCE: do not open `books/` or `v3-runs/`). You may read this edition's own closed text and the plan. The four Sol reads are in this folder (`plan-audit.md`, `source-tracking.md`, `review-editorial.md`, `review-cold.md`); read them before you touch a line. The baseline is `pre-repair-r1/` (your draft plus the nine coordinator-applied fixes in `plan-audit-apply.md`). Write `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md` when done and refresh AUTHOR-REPORT's "New canon minted" and "State at movement end".

**Verdict on the draft:** the audit ratified the structure and every piece of staging you flagged. Nothing is to be re-staged. This is a bounded line repair: joins round nine applied fixes, one paragraph of Vastin's, two short passages in chapter 26, and a handful of single lines. Keep every chapter inside the three working ranges and do not grow the movement (35,184 against ~34,000 is accepted; if anything, the cold read's item C6 will take a little out).

## A. KEPT (ruled; not to change)

Karis as the card's unnamed "bearer, one"; what Cael gave Vastin and what he withheld; the two self-readings (not counted) and the debt to tell Oryn at the mill; the hand on the gatepost as a trial; the Line at the river and the hold lifted at the first hedge; tier rights, cards, coats and the sick-house board; the dark dry-laid stone and the iron door; Cleon's appearance and terms (one term amended, B6); Oryn's two unnamed gates; the rope priced and not bought; the child's counting voice; the length. Deployment thirty-two.

## B. Already applied by the coordinator (keep the protected words exactly; smooth the joins)

1. **ch20** — the finding now reads `He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*.` (the full protected sentence). Your boulder image after it says the cord tells a man "how far it was about"; make the two agree in two or three sentences of your own (the cord round a thing gives its outside and nothing of what lies in it; what he had been writing down for it was *no length*).
2. **ch20** — "Shadow he knew by what was missing." (it was not the ninth).
3. **ch21** — `"You were the instrument," Cael said. "I was the error."` It is CAEL's line, one utterance, answering Vastin's "I had never in my life come away from a person without a figure." Check the paragraph after it.
4. **ch23** — the hands: `Karis said, "Four hands."` / `"Four," Seln said. "By count."` / `Lira said, to nobody, "Four hands," and then, "I've two."` / `Cael wrote: *Entered by count. That's the door.*` Make the join true: why Karis already has the figure four (three boxes and a case), that Cael has the book on his knee, and that Brom's and Lira's lines after it do not say the same thing again.
5. **ch23** — the close, whole (OWNER-pending #53 inside it, verbatim; the narration never repeats the figure). The join between Karis's two speeches ("She drew breath.") is the coordinator's and is yours to replace with a better one; the spoken words are fixed.
6. **ch24** — `"Off floors," Cleon said.` / `"Fused. Grey. No join. Three of them. I copied every mark on all three. Fifty-two sheets," Karis said.` / `"Fifty-two sheets," Cleon said.` She has now said "three" and "fifty-two" before his questions: re-cut the catechism after it so that he does not ask "How many places?" and she does not say "All fifty-two" as news. Cleon's lamp term now reads "One lamp goes down with you. Mine. … No other carried light…" (Movement 5 opens on a light already burning below).
7. **ch26** — after `"It's worth the look," she said. "I've looked."` now stand `"Four hands," she said.` / `"Four."` / `"Right."` — Lira, then Cael, then Lira, at the parapet when the ladder has failed to pull. Give "Four." its speaker without touching the words. The marker after the clerk's *four* is gone; close that gap.

## C. For you to recompose

1. **Why Vastin declined (ch21:143–147 and what leans on it).** Locked Book 6 (ch40:197–213; ch49:201–209) shows that he had meant to sign and that the case laid before him at Ostrand is what changed him: the office had shown him what it did with honest readings. That reason stays TRUE and CAUSAL. His private confession is an addition to it, not a replacement: he declined for a sound reason, and found that part of him was relieved never to learn what a plate would say — glad of a right act for a wrong reason. So he cannot say "I did not decline in order to spare you a plate; I declined so that I need never learn…", nor that Cael "was left a blank… so that an old man might go on supposing that he could read". Keep the two outcomes he weighed, "I could afford neither answer", the night awake, and "a better man would have been kept awake by the first". Then bring Cael's side into line (ch21:151–165: "for the sake of a man's sleep"; "I shall not take credit for where a thing fell that I dropped"), and the later mentions (ch23's cutaway; the log's "He told me why").
2. **Two tracked passages in chapter 26 (source-tracking.md): break the beat order, do not reword.** (a) ch26:55–81, Lira at the parapet: the chain is her history of being drawn upward → Cael watching for the forward lean → the lean not coming → the box. (b) ch26:183–223, the clerk: door opens → book and pen → the scan along the bench → the almost-question → the refusal to ask → *four* → admission. The report suggests another order for each; use your own if it is better, but the yard door must keep its danger (Seln's "eighty would").
3. **Wind's "ten strides" (ch20:153)** reads as a fixed ceiling. Say what it did at the cut and what it cost, and state no limit that the edition has not stated.
4. **Cleon's name (ch26's last page).** "Cleon pulled the iron to." is the first time the narration uses the name, at the climax. Let Cael read it earlier, once — on the card in Lira's hands in chapter 24, as the name Karis said at a spring — so that it is his to think at the door. Nobody says it aloud; he keeps his refusal to give it "off my own tongue".
5. **The inward inventory (editorial item 2):** nothing else to add; do not expand it.
6. **The hierarchy explained twice (cold read item 3).** In ch22–23 and ch25–26, where a scene has already shown the thing (the forges; the gate; the card), cut or compress the second telling of it in narration or dialogue. Keep every obstacle, load and choice. A few sentences in all.

## D. Standing constraints (unchanged)

Season-blind; feet, yards, miles, paces, strides, cords; no year count since the Kindling in narration; nineteen; no possessive before "Path" in narration; no new proper names (the clerk unnamed); Vastin without an age; Cael never on the square or the steps and never looking at the crown, Lira the same unasked; Lira's paper unshown; Cleon's Path not shown; no fragment acquired; the still place read round and never touched; consent explicit; every protected unit whole and once. Gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected (`bash editions/monroe-1.3/tools/ed.sh overlap book-08-before-the-paths 4`).
