# Editorial review — Movement 004, “Inside Walls”

**Verdict: REPAIR.** The movement is structurally complete and its story/canon handling is strong, but it is not yet manuscript-complete: five literal `[[TIER-B: ...]]` coordinator placeholders remain in the prose. Those omissions also create the one unprotected source-overlap hit. One progression-inventory passage needs a small numerical/canon correction, and the movement remains materially lighter and less rhythmically varied than the selected formula.

## Coverage and review stance

This is an **informed editorial review by a separate model family**, with the full compiled canon, movement brief, protected-wording map, formula instructions, supplied measurements, and overlap report. It is not a blind simulated cold read and not a real human read. Coverage is the seven supplied files, chapters 20–26, in declared order. The movement reaches its intended end at the closing of the iron door and is complete as a story unit, though the unresolved placeholders make the manuscript text incomplete.

## Unscored reader response

Interest catches immediately at `chapter-20.md:3`, “The hold came off at the first hedge.” The release of road silence turns an ordinary hedge into a rule change, and the road’s growing population makes Treswick feel present before its walls do. The inward reading at `chapter-20.md:151–205` then supplies the movement’s central discovery: Cael has mistaken the limit of his instrument for a finding about himself.

The strongest emotional passage is Vastin lowering himself onto the bank and admitting why he declined the second reading (`chapter-21.md:91–211`). Lira walking where a wall would have been (`chapter-25.md:263`) and pricing the view from the top (`chapter-26.md:65–79`) are equally effective relationship and development payoffs. Treswick’s gates, forges, boards, cards, and two doors make the city feel like one coherent machine rather than a sequence of worldbuilding stops.

Interest slips slightly during the full ten-current inventory, where several abstract sensory metaphors arrive before the reader has the new finding, and at the transitions into Oryn’s route. The cutaways are worthwhile and thematically exact, but a thirteen-year-old reader may briefly resent leaving Treswick just as its gates become immediate. The serious breaks are the five visible editorial placeholders: they remove the sentence that lands Vastin’s confession, the logic and close of Seln’s report, Cleon’s first response, and the clerk’s decisive count.

Cael matters most, but the movement makes his progress relational: Lira protects without announcing it, Vastin offers truth without asking for absolution, Karis brings all fifty-two sheets rather than curating an argument, and Cleon refuses to be told what to see. I expect the next movement to test all fifty-two sheets in the Deepstacks and to ask whether Cleon can read the construction of Cael’s still place. I would continue immediately once the placeholders are restored.

