   157	It came to the old man last, and there it stayed.
   158	
   159	It did not stay for long. It was perhaps the length of a breath. But the keeper who held it read people for his bread, and he had in front of him a porter with a stick, and a bound leg, and the lightest load of the four, who stood up under it a great deal too straight.
   160	
   161	Vastin was looking at his boots.
   162	
   163	He was doing it badly. Cael could see that he was. A person who has looked at his boots all his life lets his head hang, and this one held his at an angle, with care, as a thing that he had been told the trick of; but his right hand was shut on the ash, and it did not move an inch toward his coat.
   164	
   165	"You've a lame one," said the keeper.
   166	
   167	That was not a question either. It was Karis who took it.
   168	
   169	"He carries the light one."
   170	
   171	"He'll want to. It's a hill, from here." The feather dropped. He made a stroke on his sheet, a single one, and tore the corner off the card and gave the card back. "Go on."
   172	
   173	Vastin went under the arch with the case on his shoulder and his eyes on the setts. It was a gate that he had ridden through on four occasions, with a keeper standing at each jamb and the board held out to him, open, so that he might see for himself how it was kept; and he went through it now as one stroke in four, on the wrong side of the pen, and Cael heard him let his breath go when the shadow of the arch was behind them.
   174	
   175	Lira went under the arch second.
   176	
   177	Cael had her back in front of him, and a box on it, and above the box the back of her head. He could not see her face. He saw what her head did. As she came level with the board that hung from the second keeper's neck, where a line stood for every stranger who had walked through that gate today on his own paper, her chin turned toward it by the width of a finger.
   178	
   179	There would be names on that sheet, in a clerk's hand, each with a district and a rank; and there was a pocket in her coat with such a line folded in it, sealed, which a station at Ostrand had made out to her, and which she had worked harder for than for anything else that she owned. She had only to put the box down.
   180	
   181	Her chin came back. She shifted the beckets, and went on up the hill.
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	It was quieter inside the second wall, and it was cleaner, and for the first hundred yards that was all that he could have said of it.
   186	
   187	Then he began to see what Iron bought.
   188	
   189	The roofs were slate, every one. The street had a gutter down each side of it, with water running, and no dung. The windows had glass in them. At a corner there was a cistern with a brass spout, and a girl was filling a jug at it; and she did not put a copper in any dish, since there was no dish. A row of children's voices came out of an upper window, chanting something all together, and he caught the shape of it before the words: it was a list, and it went up by steps, and at the top of each step there was a colour.
   190	
   191	Nobody looked at them.
   192	
   193	It was not as the outer street had not looked, where everybody was carrying and a box was only a thing in the way. People here were not carrying anything. They walked with their hands free, in good cloth, at their own pace; and their eyes went over four backs under loads as eyes go over a row of posts. A woman drew her skirt aside for Brom without breaking her talk.
   194	
   195	Karis had not looked at a single slate. She walked with her face turned to the house-fronts on the upper side, where the street had been cut into the hill and the footings of the houses stood on the bluff's own rock; and twice she put out two gloved fingers, in passing, and drew them along a bed of pale stone at the height of her hip, as another person might have touched a dog.
   196	
   197	"Left foot," said Brom, to himself, on the steep of it. He was favouring two toes.
   198	
   199	The street climbed harder past the cistern. It had been laid in long shallow steps there, a stride to each, with a raised kerb for the grip of hooves. Behind him the stick had begun to come down out of time. Cael did not turn round; he slowed, by a little, as one slows a line for the last on it, and heard the breath at his shoulder catch up, and shorten.
   200	
   201	"I am aware of what you are doing," said Vastin, very low.
   202	
   203	"I'm tired. It's a hill."
   204	
   205	"It is courteous of you to be tired at my pace."
   206	
   207	There was a house on the right hand with a board beside its door.
   208	
   209	He would have gone by it. It was Lira who slowed, in front of him, with her head turned; and he read what she was reading. The board was black slate, ruled in chalk, as every board in that town was, but it had no loads on it: it had hours, and against each hour a colour. *First bell: buff. Second bell: blue.* And at the foot, in smaller letters: *Cards, after the fourth, at the side door, as the house's means allow.*
   210	
   211	There was a bench under the board. Nobody was sitting on it at that hour.
   212	
   213	"That's hers," said Lira. She did not say whose, and did not need to. "That's her column. They've hung it on a door."
   214	
   215	"It is the standing order for a district house," said Vastin. He had come up level, and stopped, with both hands on the stick, and he was looking at the board as he had looked at a bridge with five piers. "I have read it in the code, where it is the ninth article of a schedule. I had never seen it in chalk."
   216	
   217	"Does it read different in chalk?" said Brom.
   218	
   219	"It reads the same. It is a good deal harder to walk past."
   220	
   221	They walked past it. There was nothing else to be done with it. But Cael carried it up the hill with the box, a square woman's voice saying that it was not cruelty and was only how a heap was sorted; and he thought of a girl on a kerb with a basket of eggs, and a child who had taken hold of a stick with both fists.
   222	
   223	---
   224	
   225	The third wall was the oldest, and it showed.
   226	
   227	It was lower than the others, and thicker, and its stone had gone the colour of a tooth. The arch in it was round, and narrow, and not tall enough for a loaded wagon. There was one keeper under it. He was an old man, in a long pale coat that had been made for somebody broader, and he sat on a stool with a board on his knee.
   228	
   229	He read the card twice. He read it as Karis read a seal, from the stamp inward.
   230	
   231	"Lower archive," he said. He had a dry, exact voice. "I had heard that there was such a card. My father kept this gate before me, and he told me that he had passed one, once, when he was young." He looked up, at three boxes, and at a tarred case, and for rather longer at a courier's flat of waxed leather with a stamp across its face. "That is a good deal of matter."
   232	
   233	Karis said nothing. She looked at the wall.
   234	
   235	"It is not my business what it is. My business is how many." He lifted his pen, and it had no feather; he used the point. It went to her, and to Brom, and to Lira; it came to Cael, and he felt it on his breastbone like a finger, and it went on. It came to the stick, and the bound leg, and it did not linger there at all, since that old man had seen a great many porters, and he had long ago stopped expecting them to look like anything.
   236	
   237	"One, and four," he said. He made his stroke, and tore the second corner off the card.
   238	
   239	"Where do loads go?" said Karis.
   240	
   241	It was the only thing that she had been told she might ask. She asked it of the wall.
   242	
   243	"Not by the square. They'll not thank you for boxes on that paving, with the steps swept." He pointed with the pen, through the arch and to the right. "Under the wall. There is a lane. Follow it till it goes down, and then go down with it, and you will come to a yard. Mind your lame one on the steps. They are older than he is."
   244	
   245	"Thank you."
   246	
   247	"Go on."
   248	
   249	They went under the round arch, one behind another, and it was so low that Brom bent his head.
   250	
   251	*There?* asked Anchor. It asked very softly here, as a person lowers his voice in an old room. Cael told it no.
   252	
   253	Then he was through, and he stood inside the third wall of Treswick, on the crown of the bluff, with a box on his back.
   254	
   255	He had his eyes on the ground. He had put them there under the arch, before the light changed, and he held them there now as he would have held a rope. He saw setts, small ones, laid in a pattern of fans. They ran away in front of him between two rows of very old doorsteps. A long way up that street, eighty yards perhaps, they stopped being grey, and went white, where the sun came down upon something wide and open and paved.
   256	
   257	There was no sound from that end, and he had thought that there would be. There was only a brightness lying on the stones, and at the top of his sight, where he would not lift it, a paleness in the air that was not the sun's.
   258	
   259	It was a hundred paces off. He could have walked to it while a person counted to sixty. Hesk's book lay against his ribs, under the box's strap, with two sentences in it that a square woman had read with her lips moving; and he found that he was glad, for the first time, that she had made him write them. A thing that he had only said would have been arguing with him by now.
   260	
   261	He turned right.
   262	
   263	A small quick shape came up on his left side as he did it, with a box on its back, and settled there, between him and the street's end. She did not look that way herself. She had her eyes on the lane, and her chin level, and she said nothing whatever; she only walked where a wall would have been, if anybody had thought to build him one.

