    73	A road ran round the foot of the bluff, under the second wall, and he found it by going uphill until he could go no farther. It was cobbled, and it was narrow, and the houses on its upper side had been built straight against the wall's foot, so that their back rooms were the town's own masonry. He walked with one hand almost on their doors. The wall went up above the roofs on his left, pale and sheer, as high as the scree in a cut that he remembered.
    74	
    75	He came to the gate from the side.
    76	
    77	The road opened there into a small paved place, where the street from the trough came up to meet the arch. There was a cook-house built against the wall on the near hand, with smoke going up from it, and a water-butt at its corner. Cael put his shoulder against the butt and stood. Nobody looks twice at a person who is waiting beside water.
    78	
    79	*There?* said Anchor, of the arch. It was a tall one, with a groove cut down each jamb where something heavy could be let fall. He told it no.
    80	
    81	Two keepers stood in the arch, in long coats of dark blue. One had a board hung from his neck by a strap, with a sheet on it, and a pen. The other had nothing in his hands at all, and did the looking.
    82	
    83	He watched for a quarter of an hour, by the shadow of the butt, and it was as plain as a mill.
