    87	There were strangers who came by right. He saw only one, a young man in travelling boots, who had the look of a courier. He took a folded paper from his coat without being asked, and the keeper with empty hands opened it and read it all, and read out a name and a district and a rank to his fellow; and the pen wrote for as long as it takes to say a line. The young man had his paper back and went up. He had been perfectly easy. He had been written in a book as a person is who has never had cause to mind it.
    88	
