editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-008/pre-repair-r1/chapter-54.md:41:He did the other thing, which he had done in a room under a floor four days before without being asked. He stepped up beside her, and turned his left side to the wall, and bent his back, with his one good hand on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:229:She got in. For a moment, before the door shut, he saw her face with nobody looking at it, and it was the face of a woman who has known a thing for four days and is not going to say it in a stable yard. He did not understand that then, and put it away to look at later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-54.md:41:He did the other thing, which he had done in a room under a floor four days before without being asked. He stepped up beside her, and turned his left side to the wall, and bent his back, with his one good hand on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He left it so. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md:28:6. **ch43:85.** The three paragraphs that set Cael's withheld sentence beside hers are cut from the barn. Karis finishes; the others go up; she gives him the three facts. His parallel is said only on the gate-walk (43, last scene: "Karis has a thing that she won't write. I've a thing that I won't write."), and in the protected second pole. The scene's last exchange is now "I'll not ask you what the fear is." / "No. You have not, in four days, and I had noticed…" (43:109–111), with the leaf that he saw go into the box named once as his reason.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/review-cold.stdout.log:439:He knew that beat. He had tried for four days down a mountain to walk at a healer's pace, and had failed, since he went in fits, as he had always gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/review-cold.stdout.log:1426:"It's been off the tree longer than any apple on that shelf. That brown you're so fond of came down a fortnight after it, and it'll be meal before the snow's a week old." He had turned, and was reaching up to a shelf over the hearth, into the dark behind a row of crocks; and what he brought down was green, and long, and wrinkled like an old woman's knuckle. "That's the same tree as your sour one, and it's last year's. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/review-cold.stdout.log:1968:"There's a child. You told us about a child with a chest. I can keep it still for four days; I've kept it still for ten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/review-cold.stdout.log:2290:Cael had put no question to her in four days. He had seen a pen held over a clean page at first light, four days ago, from the fourth rung of a ladder, and a leaf put away with nothing on it; and he had understood then that there was a thing which she would sooner sit up with than hand to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/review-cold.stdout.log:2342:Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/review-cold.stdout.log:2424:"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/review-cold.stdout.log:2844:She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:There were eight more. One wanted a night-watch kept on the landing, and one wanted stones picked out of a field, and one was a message to be walked to a hold four days off, for a mark and feed. Most were in the same square black hand, as if one person had written them out for others; but beside the words on each somebody had drawn a little picture, a wheel or a fence or a boat with sticks standing in it, for the use of people who could make nothing of the words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:111:Then she let herself go over the other, since she had four days and nobody to see her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-60.md:131:The gap took the four days that a carter's skin had given it.
