editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/source-tracking.stdout.log:1838:     9	The carvings she kept to herself for the first four days, and he let her. He had counted the bands on the second upright at the ring and stopped; she had copied all eleven and had been going over the sheets since with a pencil and a second sheet beside each, and the second sheets were filling with marks in rows, and the rows were a tally. He knew a tally when he saw one. He did not ask what she was counting until the fourth evening, and then she said, without looking up, "The kinds. They're in no alphabet I own, and I own more than the registry prints; I said so at the ring and I've had four days to be wrong about it and I'm not. Every band is the same kind of line and never the same line, and I want to know how many kinds there are before I say anything about any of them." He asked how many. "I have a number. I'm not saying it until I've a second site to check it against. A count from one place is a count. From two it's a fact." She went back to the pencil. It was the first time in four years he had heard her refuse to say a number, and he filed that as a measurement of the number.
