editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/compiled-prompt.md:1719:**Calendar.** Sunset on the **ninety-sixth day past the Line**, at the eastern lip of the Fallow Ring's floor. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term"; "frost"; "before the snow"; "fortnight"). Days on the page: 84 (dusk), 85, 86, 87, 88, 89, 90, 91, 92, 93, 94, 95, 96; and, in Teague's window, the evening of the 89th. Weather: frost every morning; dry roads; a north wind on the Fallow. The next movement opens where this one stops.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/compiled-prompt.md:1790:- **Injuries, as fixed on the page:** Lira, left forearm bitten through between wrist and elbow, held about thirty counts, never shaken; mended in an hour at the line; eight small pink marks; slung in a strip of Thornwater's blanket, the hand under the opposite collarbone. Right ankle over outward a third time; taped on the 97th; flat-footed, whole sole. Karis, throat pressed by her own fists under the jaws; knuckles skinned; no voice on the 96th–97th; a croak on the 98th; single rasped words on the 99th. Brom, a straight cut across the pads of four fingers of ONE hand [corrected at close: the protected unit gives one hand, four finger-pads]; mended at noon on the 97th; nothing drawn until the hundredth. Cael, left calf opened along the old seam on a stone in the rut; bound by Karis in a blanket strip that stayed on until Thornwater's yard on the 99th (never bound or mended by Oryn) [corrected at close]; counting eleven strides on the 97th, about forty by the evening of the 98th, none by noon of the 99th; a pale seam "four days, counting the one it went on". Left forearm struck in the second lock; dead to the elbow that night; back to the wrist on the 99th. Ribs: the tape off in the yard on the 99th. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom on the 96th; two mendings at noon on the 97th; none on the 98th or 99th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/compiled-prompt.md:1819:**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. Three mendings on the crew in two days; none since. She has crossed the line eight times alone at dawn on the 98th, which only Cael saw. She has read him five times at the surface and once at depth, and has found movement, "more than one", and no banks. She felt something change in him in the twentieth minute; she has been told that he did it and that he must tell her what he is; **she does not know the mechanism**. Thornwater's bench is owed four days and is put off until after noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/compiled-prompt.md:1869:- **The first self-reading (flag):** two fingers on his own wrist, a palm on the breastbone; at ten, his own gait; in the long one, ten flows and no banks; near the middle, where the ten lie closest, a place that every one of them goes round and none goes through; no sound in it; nothing moves. Three countable things. Not named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/EVENT-LIST.md:135:2. **Dawn of the 98th.** Oryn at the stone alone, eight times over and back, with her own wrist, with the mule's neck held across, with her eyes shut. He has the watch; he sees all eight; he does not write it. It is hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/EVENT-LIST.md:138:6. **The yard.** The tape lifted: P "Twenty-one days. A rib is six weeks. These are done." The calf, closed in four days by her reckoning, seen and not read. P "Still and whole." Twenty minutes, the back room, first light. P "You don't get a yes. You get told." P "What does helping look like?" / "I'll know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:"I had the last one read in a quarter of an hour, three times over, and then there was nothing left of it." He turned the red-ruled square in his fingers. "I've had one letter in ninety days. Now I've two, and four days of road in front of me. I'm going to have one of them at the wash tomorrow, at the noon halt. I'll keep the other for a noon with nothing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:5:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:9:"In my time? Twice. Once to ask a guild master his mother's maiden name, which had been left off a form." The counsel's mouth moved at one corner. "Seats do not think of things late. They are very thorough about thinking of them early. But the rule stands, and we keep it, and in four days nobody will be able to say that the house was not where it was told to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:85:Cael had put no question to her in four days. He had seen a pen held over a clean page at first light, four days ago, from the fourth rung of a ladder, and a leaf put away with nothing on it; and he had understood then that there was a thing which she would sooner sit up with than hand to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:137:Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:219:"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/source-tracking.md:34:| 43:85 | “Cael had put no question to her in four days.” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:145` — “He sat at the table's end with his own book shut…” | varied | Both passages move from Karis's unwritten hypothesis to Cael's own withheld sentence, explicitly distinguish the two disciplines or sentences, then align them as two different refusals concerning the same underlying thing. Separate the comparison: let Karis finish and leave, then disclose Cael's parallel only at the gate-walk, or begin with Cael's debt and allow the reader—not Cael—to notice the resemblance later. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-53.txt:19:"Resigned in good standing." She said it without surprise. "I know the door. I have known it for four days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-53.txt:55:"So I kept it," she said. "For four days, through a return and a ruling. I let you hear the hood say revoked without knowing how short the time was. I should like to say that it was easy and that I never wavered, and I can't; I sat in a carriage this afternoon and very nearly said it through the window." Her mouth moved a little. "I wanted it to be yours. If you went, I wanted you to go because you had priced it with your own people, at a table, with my need left out of the sum. And if you stayed, I meant to walk beside you into that frame-house and take what they wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-53.txt:61:He could not answer. He had been keeping the largest thing he knew from her for half a year, and it had sat between them like a lie in every room; she had kept a thing from him for four days, and he saw now which of the two silences had been the kinder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-001/compiled-prompt.md:411:He did not ask anybody. He went round the foot of the block, where the pine had been dropping dead limbs for longer than there had been a road, and gathered them up, and broke them across his knee and laid them small to large. He was quicker at it than he had been four days ago, and he had the fire's place chosen, in the lee of the stone, before the gelding was out of the shafts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-001/compiled-prompt.md:929:- **Cael:** **nineteen** (turned on the page on the 102nd, at Thornwater). **Ten confirmed fragments.** The tenth is **Tide-adjacent** (Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; *Engagement: clinical — non-hostile*), acquired on the 100th. The anomaly is now **a location, not an entry: the still place.** It was read three times. It does not flow and does not change, and everything goes around it. It is not the Quiet and not a fragment. Not touched since a healer said *stop*. Ribs quiet again since the marsh. Calf a closed seam.
