editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:8054:    85	Cael had put no question to her in four days. He had seen a pen held over a clean page at first light, four days ago, from the fourth rung of a ladder, and a leaf put away with nothing on it; and he had understood then that there was a thing which she would sooner sit up with than hand to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:8106:   137	Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:8188:   219	"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:8602:   277	She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:8860:     9	The carvings she kept to herself for the first four days, and he let her. He had counted the bands on the second upright at the ring and stopped; she had copied all eleven and had been going over the sheets since with a pencil and a second sheet beside each, and the second sheets were filling with marks in rows, and the rows were a tally. He knew a tally when he saw one. He did not ask what she was counting until the fourth evening, and then she said, without looking up, "The kinds. They're in no alphabet I own, and I own more than the registry prints; I said so at the ring and I've had four days to be wrong about it and I'm not. Every band is the same kind of line and never the same line, and I want to know how many kinds there are before I say anything about any of them." He asked how many. "I have a number. I'm not saying it until I've a second site to check it against. A count from one place is a count. From two it's a fact." She went back to the pencil. It was the first time in four years he had heard her refuse to say a number, and he filed that as a measurement of the number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:9147:   127	"North in the morning," Oryn said. It was not an answer to Karis; it was a fact about a route. "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor. I'll be on the road at first light and I'll be three days north by the time you're back at the board." She looked at him. "Thornwater's on my route. You know where that is now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:9213:   193	*Under the rule: the rule is not for the days I want to. It's for the days I don't notice I've started. She read me for twenty minutes with my leave and could not find a bank. I could read her in twenty and find every bank she has, and she has walked past that line eleven times without stepping off the road and stepped over it eight times alone in the dark, and she is going north at first light with a route seventeen days late and my rule in her pocket. Every time. Written where the inventory goes, so that it gets read every time the inventory does.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:9238:books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-16.md:127:"North in the morning," Oryn said. It was not an answer to Karis; it was a fact about a route. "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor. I'll be on the road at first light and I'll be three days north by the time you're back at the board." She looked at him. "Thornwater's on my route. You know where that is now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:9472:     9	The carvings she kept to herself for the first four days, and he let her. He had counted the bands on the second upright at the ring and stopped; she had copied all eleven and had been going over the sheets since with a pencil and a second sheet beside each, and the second sheets were filling with marks in rows, and the rows were a tally. He knew a tally when he saw one. He did not ask what she was counting until the fourth evening, and then she said, without looking up, "The kinds. They're in no alphabet I own, and I own more than the registry prints; I said so at the ring and I've had four days to be wrong about it and I'm not. Every band is the same kind of line and never the same line, and I want to know how many kinds there are before I say anything about any of them." He asked how many. "I have a number. I'm not saying it until I've a second site to check it against. A count from one place is a count. From two it's a fact." She went back to the pencil. It was the first time in four years he had heard her refuse to say a number, and he filed that as a measurement of the number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:9759:   127	"North in the morning," Oryn said. It was not an answer to Karis; it was a fact about a route. "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor. I'll be on the road at first light and I'll be three days north by the time you're back at the board." She looked at him. "Thornwater's on my route. You know where that is now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:9825:   193	*Under the rule: the rule is not for the days I want to. It's for the days I don't notice I've started. She read me for twenty minutes with my leave and could not find a bank. I could read her in twenty and find every bank she has, and she has walked past that line eleven times without stepping off the road and stepped over it eight times alone in the dark, and she is going north at first light with a route seventeen days late and my rule in her pocket. Every time. Written where the inventory goes, so that it gets read every time the inventory does.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:11280:   163	So she walked like a duck, across a country with nothing in it but grass, at the pace of a small brown animal that never hurried and never stopped. She had hated that pace for four days on a mountain. She found now that she could not have gone faster if she had been let, and it was restful, in its way, to have the matter settled by somebody else's legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:11332:   215	*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:11458:   341	"Tomorrow. First light. The back room, with the door shut, and I'll not have eaten, so nor will you. It's both hands. It's twenty minutes, near enough; I'll not know till I'm in." She tucked the stick into her coat. "It costs me a mending, and there's a bench under that window I owe four days to. They'll keep till after noon. I've looked at every one of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:12819:    67	Dawn on the 111th: Oryn north on a route seventeen days late at Oxhollow, carrying a spare rope; the crew with her mule, three boxes and a hundred and two in hand. A rule on the page and a debt with a date. Ten fragments and one place that doesn't move. "Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three." The completion report has been on a desk somewhere for eight days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:13032:   117	- **The first self-reading (flag):** two fingers on his own wrist, a palm on the breastbone; at ten, his own gait; in the long one, ten flows and no banks; near the middle, where the ten lie closest, a place that every one of them goes round and none goes through; no sound in it; nothing moves. Three countable things. Not named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:13062:   147	10. **Apple words near the birthday.** "One was picked a month before the other"; "came down a fortnight after it"; "meal before the snow's a week old". They count apples, and carry no season name and no count to or from a birthday; I flag them in case #42/#48 is read more strictly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:13202:   319	- Ch22: Oryn: "A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone." / "Is it wrong?" / "I don't know what right would look like in you. Nobody does. That's not a comfort; it's a finding." … "Write it down and stop touching it. Things you keep touching don't heal." — Teague's healer: "Twice?" … "Then it wants binding, not hands," — Teague: "What are you." / Cael: "Why did you stop reporting?" / "Because the board pays the same whether I do or don't," … "and the registry doesn't, and I'd sooner be worth what I did than what they wrote." … "That's not an answer to mine." / "No." / "You didn't answer." / "I don't have one. Neither does the board. You said so." / "I did." … "The board's going to need a new column."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:13218:   335	| The still place | Located (M6, unnamed); named as *the still place* (M8); read three times; "Not the Quiet. Not a fragment." | *source, before, primordial, recurrence* (Book 9's word); a Rune-like "shape with bones" (Book 8's); any theory, including Oryn's |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14060:   285	"It's been off the tree longer than any apple on that shelf. That brown you're so fond of came down a fortnight after it, and it'll be meal before the snow's a week old." He had turned, and was reaching up to a shelf over the hearth, into the dark behind a row of crocks; and what he brought down was green, and long, and wrinkled like an old woman's knuckle. "That's the same tree as your sour one, and it's last year's. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14194:285:He knew that beat. He had tried for four days down a mountain to walk at a healer's pace, and had failed, since he went in fits, as he had always gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14269:285:"It's been off the tree longer than any apple on that shelf. That brown you're so fond of came down a fortnight after it, and it'll be meal before the snow's a week old." He had turned, and was reaching up to a shelf over the hearth, into the dark behind a row of crocks; and what he brought down was green, and long, and wrinkled like an old woman's knuckle. "That's the same tree as your sour one, and it's last year's. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14330:119:"There's a child. You told us about a child with a chest. I can keep it still for four days; I've kept it still for ten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14369:85:Cael had put no question to her in four days. He had seen a pen held over a clean page at first light, four days ago, from the fourth rung of a ladder, and a leaf put away with nothing on it; and he had understood then that there was a thing which she would sooner sit up with than hand to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14374:137:Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14387:219:"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14441:277:She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14505:   137	Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14758:   285	He knew that beat. He had tried for four days down a mountain to walk at a healer's pace, and had failed, since he went in fits, as he had always gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14938:9-- POV: Cael ≈29,900. **Teague cutaway ≈1,700** (days 117–120: following the crew's trail three days along a bearing he does not understand, the healer brought because a shoulder was mended once; sitting down beside the boy; the column). **Karis cutaway ≈2,000** (days 119–120 at the far fire: three copies, the ledger, the edge laid and moved; it ends exactly on the pencil stopping; no who or why). **Seln cutaway ≈1,600** (days 124–128, the road home: the ledger in his coat; giving it to Karis and not asking for it back). **Oryn cutaway ≈2,800** (day 122 to about 128: the gap north-west, four days, three weeks late; Thornwater; the old woman's knee; writing the letter, ending on its four lines).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14958:29:- **Two days at the marsh (119–120):** two fires. Seln's blanket over Karis's shoulders. Lira keeps the bolt in the bow. Brom: "Twice." / "Twice." Oryn sleeps in front of people. **The second self-reading** (an hour; the banks a limit; **the still place**, named here for the first time; the river round a stone it has always known; four things he can count; protected log). The third before dawn: "the same." **Oryn changes the order**: "You in me." Asked and granted; surface, then a minute of the long one at her count, and while he is in, **he tells her exactly**; her finding (protected); "Write it down and stop touching it." **C3: Karis lays the cord at the Hall's perimeter** in the marsh between copies. **Teague's crew** along the track with a fifth: the older healer of no Path, "Twice?" / "Then it wants binding, not hands." The two women bind it. **Teague sits down beside him**, as Brom once did. "What are you." **The wrist a foot from his hand:** not the short reading, not the long one, not the asking. "Why did you stop reporting?" (protected exchange). "The board's going to need a new column." The log (protected). **The pencil stopping** at the far fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14994:461-- **The two HOLD slips (flag):** one bark and one shingle; a bull to be brought down off top grazing before the snow, and a ford to be staked. Each hold "asked who". They will wait a fortnight for the five and then have Teague. Cael tells Pike on the 91st not to keep them past the fortnight. **They are still on the oak at the end of the movement.**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:14999:487:**Calendar.** Sunset on the **ninety-sixth day past the Line**, at the eastern lip of the Fallow Ring's floor. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term"; "frost"; "before the snow"; "fortnight"). Days on the page: 84 (dusk), 85, 86, 87, 88, 89, 90, 91, 92, 93, 94, 95, 96; and, in Teague's window, the evening of the 89th. Weather: frost every morning; dry roads; a north wind on the Fallow. The next movement opens where this one stops.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:15054:558:- **Injuries, as fixed on the page:** Lira, left forearm bitten through between wrist and elbow, held about thirty counts, never shaken; mended in an hour at the line; eight small pink marks; slung in a strip of Thornwater's blanket, the hand under the opposite collarbone. Right ankle over outward a third time; taped on the 97th; flat-footed, whole sole. Karis, throat pressed by her own fists under the jaws; knuckles skinned; no voice on the 96th–97th; a croak on the 98th; single rasped words on the 99th. Brom, a straight cut across the pads of four fingers of ONE hand [corrected at close: the protected unit gives one hand, four finger-pads]; mended at noon on the 97th; nothing drawn until the hundredth. Cael, left calf opened along the old seam on a stone in the rut; bound by Karis in a blanket strip that stayed on until Thornwater's yard on the 99th (never bound or mended by Oryn) [corrected at close]; counting eleven strides on the 97th, about forty by the evening of the 98th, none by noon of the 99th; a pale seam "four days, counting the one it went on". Left forearm struck in the second lock; dead to the elbow that night; back to the wrist on the 99th. Ribs: the tape off in the yard on the 99th. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom on the 96th; two mendings at noon on the 97th; none on the 98th or 99th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:15078:587:**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. Three mendings on the crew in two days; none since. She has crossed the line eight times alone at dawn on the 98th, which only Cael saw. She has read him five times at the surface and once at depth, and has found movement, "more than one", and no banks. She felt something change in him in the twentieth minute; she has been told that he did it and that he must tell her what he is; **she does not know the mechanism**. Thornwater's bench is owed four days and is put off until after noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/plan-audit.stdout.log:15437:   219	"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
