editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:41:He did not ask anybody. He went round the foot of the block, where the pine had been dropping dead limbs for longer than there had been a road, and gathered them up, and broke them across his knee and laid them small to large. He was quicker at it than he had been four days ago, and he had the fire's place chosen, in the lee of the stone, before the gelding was out of the shafts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:65:He lay still on the scrubbed wood and looked at the underside of a turf roof. Nine things lay in him as quiet as he had ever known them; he had held them for eighty-four days, and it had become very like breathing. He could not tell what her fingers were reaching for. He knew only that none of the nine had stirred, and that if one had, he would have felt her feel it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:213:So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair/chapter-44.md:277:She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1582:- **Body clocks agree.** Ribs broken on day 78 and untaped on day 99 are twenty-one days, with eighteen days actually taped (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:309-325`; `BOOK_MAP.md:222`). The calf opens day 96 and is closed day 99—“four days, counting the one it went on” (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:265-329`). Brom's string ban runs from day 97 through the first allowable day 100 (`manuscript/chapter-34.md:139-143`). Cael is whole on the 99th, so first light on the 100th is the first day Oryn's own rule permits the long reading (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:321-339`).
