editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:13849:    65	He lay still on the scrubbed wood and looked at the underside of a turf roof. Nine things lay in him as quiet as he had ever known them; he had held them for eighty-four days, and it had become very like breathing. He could not tell what her fingers were reaching for. He knew only that none of the nine had stirred, and that if one had, he would have felt her feel it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/pre-repair-author/chapter-49.md:213:That was the whole of it. Five words had gone out from a desk by a noon dispatch, after a man had weighed them for four days on a post road and found them worth sending though they would stop nothing. They had stopped nothing. They were in the minute as an absence, between the count of the exhibits and the count of the orders, and the clerk was already reading the next line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair-r1/chapter-43.md:85:Cael had put no question to her in four days. He had seen a pen held over a clean page at first light, four days ago, from the fourth rung of a ladder, and a leaf put away with nothing on it; and he had understood then that there was a thing which she would sooner sit up with than hand to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair-r1/chapter-43.md:137:Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair-r1/chapter-43.md:219:"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:109:The healer did not answer at once. He saw her look at his right hand, where it hung, and then at her own wrist; and for the space of one breath there was the hunger in her face that he had seen at a ford and in a yard, when she had wanted to know a thing more than she wanted her supper. She put it by as he had seen her put it by before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:127:"She looked at your hand before she said it," Karis murmured. Her voice was pitched for his ear alone. "And then at her own wrist. I have seen that look on a person in front of a shut press. She was curious about you. Enter that somewhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-44.txt:75:They had spent four days on that table of renewals, in the annex, at the start of the year. He had found the nine days himself, in his own old Log, and Karis had let him find them. And a man in a mended coat had just stood up before a hood and said that every line of it was right, and thrown away a piece of his own petition rather than leave an error standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-40.txt:249:He had come back the night before from the river stations by the post road. He had walked all six in four days. Every frame was sound, though one had been shimmed and would read the damp no longer, and he had written that it would read the damp no longer, and signed it. His left hand had disliked the mornings on the river very much. His clerk had had a fire lit in the room before he came in, without being asked, which the clerk did perhaps twice a year, and had said nothing about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair-r1/chapter-42.md:119:"There's a child. You told us about a child with a chest. I can keep it still for four days; I've kept it still for ten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/review-cold-prompt.md:1475:So she walked like a duck, across a country with nothing in it but grass, at the pace of a small brown animal that never hurried and never stopped. She had hated that pace for four days on a mountain. She found now that she could not have gone faster if she had been let, and it was restful, in its way, to have the matter settled by somebody else's legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/review-cold-prompt.md:1527:*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/review-cold-prompt.md:1651:"Tomorrow. First light. The back room, with the door shut, and I'll not have eaten, so nor will you. It's both hands. It's twenty minutes, near enough; I'll not know till I'm in." She tucked the stick into her coat. "It costs me a mending, and there's a bench under that window I owe four days to. They'll keep till after noon. I've looked at every one of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-43.txt:11:Lira was at her chalk at the south end with her back to the door. She had taped her own wrists but not the ankle, because the healer had said there was nothing left in it to tape, and she stood now with her weight even on both feet and her left arm tucked a little toward her ribs, from the old habit, and did not look round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-43.txt:165:She had started it in the dairy week. Not that night; the night after, when she could not sleep for thinking about three carters and a road with four days of stamps on it and then none. She had thought then, lying on her back in the dark, about what she actually owned. Not the program. The program was Rooke's, and the east hall was the house's, and the red book belonged to a river academy and was only lent. The figure on the steward's sheet was hers, but it was hers the way a room is yours when somebody else holds the key; it meant something because a hall's seal was on it, and it would go on meaning something exactly as far as that seal was honoured.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-001/EVENT-LIST.md:41:- **Fourteen slips on day 9** (mine): the landing broker's six crossings (two marks); a fence-line at a steading on the north water (four and flour); a well on the south road (four; no detail given — Movement 2 owns the hazard); roof shingles at the upper inn; fleece carried down from a north hold; a strayed mule; a night-watch on the landing; stone-picking; a message to a hold four days off; and others. None says what tier or rank the crew must be. That is the missing thing Cael reads for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-006/pre-repair-r1/chapter-40.md:285:"It's been off the tree longer than any apple on that shelf. That brown you're so fond of came down a fortnight after it, and it'll be meal before the snow's a week old." He had turned, and was reaching up to a shelf over the hearth, into the dark behind a row of crocks; and what he brought down was green, and long, and wrinkled like an old woman's knuckle. "That's the same tree as your sour one, and it's last year's. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/pre-repair-author/chapter-53.md:17:"*Resigned in good standing.*" She said it without surprise. "I know the door. I have known it for four days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/pre-repair-author/chapter-53.md:53:"So I kept it," she said. "For four days, through a return and a ruling. I let you hear the hood say *revoked* without knowing how short the time was. I should like to say that it was easy and that I never wavered, and I can't; I sat in a carriage this afternoon and very nearly said it through the window." Her mouth moved a little. "I wanted it to be yours. If you went, I wanted you to go because you had priced it with your own people, at a table, with my need left out of the sum. And if you stayed, I meant to walk beside you into that frame-house and take what they wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/pre-repair-author/chapter-53.md:59:He found that he could not answer. He had been keeping the largest thing he knew from her for half a year, and it had sat between them like a lie in every room; she had kept a thing from him for four days, and he saw now which of the two silences had been the kinder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-50.txt:7:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/editions/eleven-v4-directors-cut/chapters/chapter-50.txt:11:"In my time? Twice. Once to ask a guild master his mother's maiden name, which had been left off a form." The counsel's mouth moved at one corner. "Seats do not think of things late. They are very thorough about thinking of them early. But the rule stands, and we keep it, and in four days nobody will be able to say that the house was not where it was told to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:73:They had spent four days on that table of renewals, in the annex, at the start of the year. He had found the nine days himself, in his own old Log, and Karis had let him find them. And a man in a mended coat had just stood up before a hood and said that every line of it was right, and thrown away a piece of his own petition rather than leave an error standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1395:354:| 42–57 | ch7 | Redrafted whole. Changes, in order: <ul><li>The counsel: "I've looked tonight for the case where somebody turned these sections round to point at the man standing in the frame… This is new."</li><li>"Do you know the difference, in front of a seat, between a description and an evaluation?"</li><li>Bracken reads "flat"; the clerks' first-year joke ("the only page in the code nobody has ever had to stamp"); "imagined you" kept.</li><li>Withrow's morning after is rebuilt around the ferry, the guild master and the sealed file ("Kept, every page, perfectly safe, and nobody may open it"); "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post."; "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow… The second had a daughter."</li><li>The calendar: three silent marks read out by Karis ("The first. The eighth. The twenty-second."), with "in thirteen years I have never seen a seat take those three words back for anybody's lame horse".</li><li>The six slips are spoken as their words only, with one comment at the fifth.</li><li>The not-a-trick paragraph is now "Bracken would carry a paper like this. So would I… Look for the trick all night and you'll come up empty."</li><li>"the other choice has its own word in the docket, and the word is *fled*… I'll have bones on it in four days."</li><li>The stratum lead-in: "That clause is why you've never stood in a station since Denvash."</li><li>The note goes inside the front cover; the question mark: "She would answer it in her own time."</li><li>Seln's lamp: Cael "did not let himself guess what the man at the copying table was making".</li><li>Karis's instructions are cut to "Four days" and "Thank you for the tea".</li><li>The telling covers what moved the table, in a new order. Brom: "Then what's the answer for?" Cael's answer names the warden, provost and tournament himself; Lira: "And this time?… What if the people didn't fail this time?"; Lira leaves without the "Bed" line; Brom's second "Corridor." comes at the door.</li><li>Pole frame: "The entry was finished before the pen touched paper."</li><li>The prolepsis is trimmed to "It looked like the truth, because, as far as he could see from that window, it was."</li><li>The lamps go out by the hall's own count (the counter pair, the table's pairs from the far end, the press lamp last).</li></ul> |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1406:462:**Calendar.** No season, month or English weekday names. Day 9 Second-day: drafting begins (four days to bones). Day 12 Fifth-day: bones. Day 13 night: the integrity section. Day 16: the counter. Two nights of Bracken's reading; the first rain; the weighing. **Day 19, Fifth-day: the answer goes down, three days inside the time** (due day 22). **Day 23: the seat's notice. Day 25, Fourth-day: Jent's conference; Withrow's office that evening.** Day 26 Fifth-day: board line; wing three. Day 27: the coats placed; the honest floor. **Day 30, Second-day: Lira's trial.** Day 31: the district docket's confirmation and the Velmere letter; opened the third evening (day 33). Day 35: Karis's budget; the frames days 35–38. Day 39: the annex convening. **Day 40, Fifth-day (the year's sixth): the first sitting.** **Day 42, Seventh-day: the month's close.** Lira's first meet off the hill is about four weeks away (Rooke's board). Brom's panel and the ruling share the year's last Fifth-day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1442:643:- (e) The Seln circuit is walked on days 48 (post sort) and 49 (lodge), and the four queries, Hesk's letter, the inventory and Withrow's story all fall on day 49, so the inventory is named inside its sixth week. The source's "four days" between the queries and Hesk's letter became two hours.
