editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:3270:    47	He could do the second tonight. Four people were asleep or near it, and the fire was low. He had his fingers on his own wrist before he saw what he was about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:3509:    59	"He's just told you what it is. It's got a ruled edge and a word for itself. I don't need it read to me a third time." Her right hand had closed on the knot. "There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts. There's a fence at forty, the day the hold comes off it. That's ninety marks of work that was posted by people who want a fence. We'd be down from both before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:3913:    59	"He's just told you what it is. It's got a ruled edge and a word for itself. I don't need it read to me a third time." Her right hand had closed on the knot. "There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts. There's a fence at forty, the day the hold comes off it. That's ninety marks of work that was posted by people who want a fence. We'd be down from both before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:4147:   109	The healer did not answer at once. He saw her look at his right hand, where it hung, and then at her own wrist; and for the space of one breath there was the hunger in her face that he had seen at a ford and in a yard, when she had wanted to know a thing more than she wanted her supper. She put it by as he had seen her put it by before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:4165:   127	"She looked at your hand before she said it," Karis murmured. Her voice was pitched for his ear alone. "And then at her own wrist. I have seen that look on a person in front of a shut press. She was curious about you. Enter that somewhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:4456:   167	One by one they came down and tried it, since no one of them would take another's word for such a thing: Lira, and Brom, and Seln, each a stride past the stone and a stride back. The healer went over last, with two fingers on her own wrist and her lips moving, and came out again and nodded. Cael walked across beside Brom, and felt what he had felt on the Fallow, which was grass under his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:4713:   133	She ran *down*. She went away from the stone, and from the two mules, and from the only ground on which a Path would answer, straight down the middle of the old road toward a bare grey floor with a hole in it. She ran on the whole of her foot, slapping, like a child in its father's boots; a healer had told her four days ago that a certain bone was her own again, and she was using it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:5868:   291	There was one thing more, and she had kept it for the last of the oil. She had been over the Fallow's eleven strips for four days at a trestle in a barn, and she had every stroke of them by heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:5872:   295	"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6174:    53	"Seven holds owe me and I owe them. That's the route. I'm at the third, and the fourth is four days on, and it's late already, because I sat at Thornwater for a week and a half with a crew that's not on my circuit." One finger, on her knee. "A crew's walking into a floor where my Path dies. I know what that floor does. I stood at its line eight times alone in the dark to be sure, and I was sure the first time." Two. "The one patient I've never once been able to read is going in first. He says nothing on that floor does anything to him. I've seen it. I've had my hands in him. I still can't read him, and I'm the only healer in a country, and he's going in *first*." Three. "And if I go with you, Thornwater's next visit is eleven days late. The circuit's twenty days round, and I've spent a week and a half of it at one table, and the day south and the two back go on top. Eleven days, at the far end, where I can't see it. There's an old woman at every hold, near enough, and somebody at Thornwater might die of eleven days." Four. She put the hand flat. "That's the whole of it. I've said it all so you've heard it all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6377:   103	"Forty-one kinds of line. The Ring has forty-one. I counted them for four days and wouldn't say the number, because a count from one place is a count. The Stair has forty-one." She picked up the tally and held it so he could see the strokes, which he could not read and did not need to. "Every kind that's on those eleven uprights is on these four walls. Every one. Not one kind here that isn't there, and not one there that isn't here — except one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6381:   107	"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred meters and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6468:    55	Three hundred. The Fallow one had been a hundred, dry, and had been the best number on the board by forty. This was three times that, for a site one day south instead of four days east, and it hung on a nail where every crew on the wall had read it and put it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7113:   111	"No. You have not, in four days, and I had noticed." She took her hand from the lid. "It is the kindest thing that anybody has done for me since I crossed the Line, and I have entered it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7129:   127	Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7207:   205	"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7444:   107	"North in the morning," Oryn said. It was not an answer to Karis; it was a fact about a route. "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor. I'll be on the road at first light and I'll be three days north by the time you're back at the board." She looked at him. "Thornwater's on my route. You know where that is now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7616:   279	She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-007/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8277:+There was one thing more, and she had kept it for the last of the oil. She had been over the Fallow's eleven strips for four days at a trestle in a barn, and she had every stroke of them by heart.
