editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/plan-audit.stdout.log:19404:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:163:So she walked like a duck, across a country with nothing in it but grass, at the pace of a small brown animal that never hurried and never stopped. She had hated that pace for four days on a mountain. She found now that she could not have gone faster if she had been let, and it was restful, in its way, to have the matter settled by somebody else's legs.
