editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred, which was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:149:All of those were his, and he saw that each of them was a way of dressing the plain thing so that it should look better on him. The plain thing was that five people had a hundred and eight marks and the cold coming, and that this paid as much again for four days of road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:245:"So we could go four days and come home with a line and no money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:131:"Then it stays shut," said Brom. "We've walked four days for a line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:637:That was the whole of it. Five words had gone out from a desk by a noon dispatch, after a man had weighed them for four days on a post road and found them worth sending though they would stop nothing. They had stopped nothing. They were in the minute as an absence, between the count of the exhibits and the count of the orders, and the clerk was already reading the next line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:693:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:697:"In my time? Twice. Once to ask a guild master his mother's maiden name, which had been left off a form." The counsel's mouth moved at one corner. "Seats do not think of things late. They are very thorough about thinking of them early. But the rule stands, and we keep it, and in four days nobody will be able to say that the house was not where it was told to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:1001:That was the whole of it. Five words had gone out from a desk by a noon dispatch, after a man had weighed them for four days on a post road and found them worth sending though they would stop nothing. They had stopped nothing. They were in the minute as an absence, between the count of the exhibits and the count of the orders, and the clerk was already reading the next line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:1061:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:1065:"In my time? Twice. Once to ask a guild master his mother's maiden name, which had been left off a form." The counsel's mouth moved at one corner. "Seats do not think of things late. They are very thorough about thinking of them early. But the rule stands, and we keep it, and in four days nobody will be able to say that the house was not where it was told to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:1603:She got in. For a moment, before the door shut, he saw her face with nobody looking at it, and it was the face of a woman who has known a thing for four days and is not going to say it in a stable yard. He did not understand that then, and put it away to look at later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:2035:"*Resigned in good standing.*" She said it without surprise. "I know the door. I have known it for four days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:2071:"So I kept it," she said. "For four days, through a return and a ruling. I let you hear the hood say *revoked* without knowing how short the time was. I should like to say that it was easy and that I never wavered, and I can't; I sat in a carriage this afternoon and very nearly said it through the window." Her mouth moved a little. "I wanted it to be yours. If you went, I wanted you to go because you had priced it with your own people, at a table, with my need left out of the sum. And if you stayed, I meant to walk beside you into that frame-house and take what they wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/review-cold.stdout.log:2081:He found that he could not answer. He had been keeping the largest thing he knew from her for half a year, and it had sat between them like a lie in every room; she had kept a thing from him for four days, and he saw now which of the two silences had been the kinder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:33:"It ends here," she said. "At about this fence, or an hour beyond; the plate isn't fine enough to say. I have carried this sheet for ninety-four days in case it should be of use, and from this gate on it is a clean piece of paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:109:Then she let herself go over the other, since she had four days and nobody to see her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:129:The gap took the four days that a carter's skin had given it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:151:She was a very small old woman. Oryn had mended that knee at a mill, thirteen days before, and had seen its owner go away up a valley on a hurdle between two grandsons. She came over the low place every year before the snow, to her daughter's hearth in this long house; and Oryn had told her, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee here, after the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:161:Oryn got down on her heels in the frost, and put both hands round the joint. Her lips went to three. It was all there: the old banks, worn as smooth as a doorstep, and the current going through the place where a ladder had been as though no ladder had ever stood in it. She had promised that knee a look after the thaw, and it had walked over a hill to be looked at before the snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:289:"Then here's the last of what I've got, and it's a healer's and not a theorist's. Write it down and stop touching it. Things you keep touching don't heal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/pre-repair-r2/chapter-25.md:179:She had not finished the count. She found she did not want to finish it out loud, even to herself; the bottom of it was a place four days east of Ostrand where the stamps stopped, and past that there was no steward and no sheet and no counter in Ostrand with her file in a drawer, and no Silver card. She had asked the question in the dairy and meant it. She still meant it. She was not going to say the rest of it tonight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1475:So she walked like a duck, across a country with nothing in it but grass, at the pace of a small brown animal that never hurried and never stopped. She had hated that pace for four days on a mountain. She found now that she could not have gone faster if she had been let, and it was restful, in its way, to have the matter settled by somebody else's legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-005/review-editorial-prompt.md:1527:*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
