editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof-part3.stdout.log:12296:51-- **Body clocks agree.** Ribs broken on day 78 and untaped on day 99 are twenty-one days, with eighteen days actually taped (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:309-325`; `BOOK_MAP.md:222`). The calf opens day 96 and is closed day 99—“four days, counting the one it went on” (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:265-329`). Brom's string ban runs from day 97 through the first allowable day 100 (`manuscript/chapter-34.md:139-143`). Cael is whole on the 99th, so first light on the 100th is the first day Oryn's own rule permits the long reading (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:321-339`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof-part3.stdout.log:14527:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/STATE_LEDGER.md:409:**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, said by herself. She knows that she cannot read Cael, by surface reading, hurt or whole, and that his ribs knit "with nothing doing it"; she knows nothing of fragments and has no theory. She has promised him the long reading "when you're not broken". **Her sum (her window, the 92nd):** whole on the 99th by the rule, though her hands say sooner; the long reading on the 100th at the earliest; her Thornwater stop ends on the 95th; she means to work four days more at Thornwater's table and has put one day on the route, for the reading. She is five days behind her round. She has mended six times on the page: Lira's leg and Brom's wrist (80th); a scald, a foot and a collarbone (84th); and, in her window, a shoulder at two mendings' cost (86th). She keeps no record. She told Teague on the hill only that she was late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof-part3.stdout.log:14583:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/pre-B6-reconciliation/BOOK_MAP.md:139:**Two hard rules for every window:** (1) **No member of Teague's crew is named, ever**, including in Teague's own head. Book 8 kills "the young one" at the pass and says his name once without entering it. Teague thinks of his people as the spear, the bow, the boy and the healer, which is how a captain counts. (2) **Oryn's holds stay unnamed** except Oxhollow, Thornwater and Millrace. In her head they are what they owe her or what is there: the hold with the child; the hold that gave the coat; the fourth, four days on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-cold-prompt.md:1328:*The brass came up from the coast at last, and it is a third dearer than last year and not as good, and the founder says it is nobody's fault, which I believe, because it never is. I have a surveyor's level on the bench that came back to me after thirty years. I made it for the man's father. The bubble has wandered a hair, and the son brought it in to be put right; and then, when I had it in pieces, he asked me to set it back the way it was, because his father laid out every boundary in the valley with it a hair wrong and the whole valley is built to agree with it now. I spent four days deciding. In the end I did as he asked. I am telling you this because you will have an opinion, and I should like to hear it before I change my mind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-49.md:133:She ran *down*. She went away from the stone, and from the two mules, and from the only ground on which a Path would answer, straight down the middle of the old road toward a bare grey floor with a hole in it. She ran on the whole of her foot, slapping, like a child in its father's boots; a healer had told her four days ago that a certain bone was her own again, and she was using it.
