editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log:3731:   837	- **The road beyond the Hall (flag):** a made road an hour east of the marsh, with holds on it (two shut for the cold term, a third long dead, with one gable standing); the hide carters use it; it forks at a heap of chock-stones about a day on. The left hand climbs to a notch between two hills and is the gap to Thornwater, four days, coming down onto the Fallow road and in by Thornwater's eastern gate. The right hand goes on under a long bare ridge, "a great whale's back", a mile broad, which ends in a crest above the fourth site.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log:3737:   843	- **Oryn, days 122–128 (flag):** four days through the gap with the jenny; Thornwater at dusk on the 125th; the bowman's two fingers, for Lira. **The old woman of the hurdle winters at Thornwater, at her daughter's hearth; Oryn had told her at the mill, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee there after the thaw.** She rested it ten days, as bid, and then walked the day and a half over the low place in two, with a grandson to carry the bread; she is first on the bench on the 126th; Oryn reads the knee (mended at Millrace on the 113th) and it has held. Seven on the bench, two mendings. Word by the stooped carter on the 127th: the mill a crushed thumb; two gates no burials; at the ford a finger lost to a chain and the hand kept; the child's breathing no different. On the evening of the 128th the steading's woman gives her bark, ink and a quill; the carter goes down at first light on the 129th and will not come up again before the thaw. She means to go north by the wicket when the bench is paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/whole-arc-read.stdout.log:3745:   851	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven** (the ford 1; the Ring 12; Oryn on the ridge 1; the Stair 9; the Hall 3; Oryn at the marsh 1). The trial point at the Hall's kerb on the 118th is logged and not counted. Shadow used twice in all and shut. Anchor: looked three times; unbound once (the Stair); a point set twice, of which one is the uncounted trial and one held the breach. Tide: Oryn twice, each time by her leave; himself three times (102, 119, 120); nobody else; never inside a line. **The still place:** his old log label, now a location under the breastbone, read three times; nothing moves in it; all ten go round it and none through; they do not pile against it; not the Quiet; not a fragment; told to Oryn at once and in order on the 120th, and to the other four in three sentences on the 121st; he has been told to write it down and stop touching it, and has not touched it since. Body: ribs quiet since the morning of the 129th; right forearm and left palm no longer remarked; calf a closed seam. Hesk's satchel: the log (the entries of the 118th, 119th, 120th, the fork, the crest, and the inventory of the 129th), the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter and note, Ephram's and Vell's letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark, and Oryn's letter on bark. **He has Hesk's brass straightedge, and it reads true in his hand.** He has said "Noted" to Teague three times. He has not answered Ephram.
