editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/listening-proof.stdout.log:7284:chapter-40.md:285: "It's been off the tree longer than any apple on that shelf. That brown you're so fond of came down a fortnight after it, and it'll be meal before the snow's a week old." He had turned, and was reaching up to a shelf over the hearth, into the dark behind a row of crocks; and what he brought down was green, and long, and wrinkled like an old woman's knuckle. "That's the same tree as your sour one, and it's last year's. Go on."
