editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-007/review-cold.stdout.log:1204:She had started it in the dairy week. Not that night; the night after, when she could not sleep for thinking about three carters and a road with four days of stamps on it and then none. She had thought then, lying on her back in the dark, about what she actually owned. Not the program. The program was Rooke's, and the east hall was the house's, and the red book belonged to a river academy and was only lent. The figure on the steward's sheet was hers, but it was hers the way a room is yours when somebody else holds the key; it meant something because a hall's seal was on it, and it would go on meaning something exactly as far as that seal was honoured.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-007/review-cold.stdout.log:1368:He looked for it, quite honestly. They had spent four days on that table of renewals, in the annex, at the start of the year. He had found the nine days himself, in his own old Log, and Karis had let him find them. He had been proud of it for a week. And a man in a mended coat had just stood up before a hood and said that every line of it was right, and adopted it, and thrown away a piece of his own petition rather than leave an error standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-007/review-cold.stdout.log:2194:He knew what would be done with it. The code let a seat proceed upon its own authority where a certificate was withheld; he had cited that section himself, once, long ago, when a guild hall's assessor would not sign. His line would stop nothing. The sittings would sit, and the ruling would be read, and somewhere in a minute a clerk would enter that a thing usually present was not there. That was the whole of what he could do, and he had weighed it on a post road for four days and found that it was still worth doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-007/review-cold.stdout.log:2653:He knew what would be done with it. The code let a seat proceed upon its own authority where a certificate was withheld; he had cited that section himself, once, long ago, when a guild hall's assessor would not sign. His line would stop nothing. The sittings would sit, and the ruling would be read, and somewhere in a minute a clerk would enter that a thing usually present was not there. That was the whole of what he could do, and he had weighed it on a post road for four days and found that it was still worth doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-45.md:47:He could do the second tonight. Four people were asleep or near it, and the fire was low. He had his fingers on his own wrist before he saw what he was about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred, which was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-25.md:149:All of those were his, and he saw that each of them was a way of dressing the plain thing so that it should look better on him. The plain thing was that five people had a hundred and eight marks and the cold coming, and that this paid as much again for four days of road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-25.md:245:"So we could go four days and come home with a line and no money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:1258:    44	- **Day 81 onward:** tape, explained ("It tells the ribs where the edge is"); the lip, thirty-one paces; **four days down** with her walk catalogued (carter's pace; the down-slope side of the mule; the raw heel taped, because she can't heal herself). **Field care taught** (Lira learns to tape her own ankle properly; Brom learns what a wrapped wrist allows). **The ankle refused** on the arithmetic: "That's a tape and a season." **The route told** at the second night's fire: seven, twenty days, thirty-odd rounds, late eleven times and now twelve, what each owes and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; "I remember who I've had my hands on… That's the book."; the city's list by tier; the want (protected).
