editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:2467:    55	- **Road times (prose, fixed):** Lowmarch ↔ Thornwater two days; Thornwater → the Fallow rise two days; Lowmarch → Oxhollow six days (north-west); Lowmarch → Millrace one day south on the Stair road; Millrace's fork → the Stair's line the same afternoon; the Stair → the Hall three days along the bearing; the Hall → the fork one day; the fork → the fourth site a day and a half; the fourth site → Lowmarch six days; the fourth site's gap → Thornwater four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:2820:    44	- **Day 81 onward:** tape, explained ("It tells the ribs where the edge is"); the lip, thirty-one paces; **four days down** with her walk catalogued (carter's pace; the down-slope side of the mule; the raw heel taped, because she can't heal herself). **Field care taught** (Lira learns to tape her own ankle properly; Brom learns what a wrapped wrist allows). **The ankle refused** on the arithmetic: "That's a tape and a season." **The route told** at the second night's fire: seven, twenty days, thirty-odd rounds, late eleven times and now twelve, what each owes and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; "I remember who I've had my hands on… That's the book."; the city's list by tier; the want (protected).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:3054:   160	**Cael.** Eighteen. **Nine confirmed fragments and the uncounted anomaly.** **Deployment since the Line: one** (the ford burst, day 53). Nothing was used on the mountain or since: Anchor, Compression, the Iron read, Wind and Storm all offered and were refused; Shadow sealed. **Three ribs broken, left side, low, on the 78th; taped with five bands on the 81st; twenty-one days from the break, and Oryn means to lift the tape herself.** Left calf opened along the old bite on the 78th, bound in Oryn's linen, not mended. No log entries for the 78th and 79th. He has been read three times by a healer (the 80th, and twice on the 84th) and has answered three questions truly: his hurts knit in the right places; faster than other people's; no healer has ever got anything from him. He has not asked Oryn south. He has not answered Hesk's letter. Teague has spoken to him and he has said one word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:8156:    44	- **Day 81 onward:** tape, explained ("It tells the ribs where the edge is"); the lip, thirty-one paces; **four days down** with her walk catalogued (carter's pace; the down-slope side of the mule; the raw heel taped, because she can't heal herself). **Field care taught** (Lira learns to tape her own ankle properly; Brom learns what a wrapped wrist allows). **The ankle refused** on the arithmetic: "That's a tape and a season." **The route told** at the second night's fire: seven, twenty days, thirty-odd rounds, late eleven times and now twelve, what each owes and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; "I remember who I've had my hands on… That's the book."; the city's list by tier; the want (protected).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:10174:    65	He lay still on the scrubbed wood and looked at the underside of a turf roof. Nine things lay in him as quiet as he had ever known them; he had held them for eighty-four days, and it had become very like breathing. He could not tell what her fingers were reaching for. He knew only that none of the nine had stirred, and that if one had, he would have felt her feel it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:10540:    15	Teague's crew was gone on the hold contracts, a week out and a week back, and Oxhollow's slip had come in on a carter behind them and sat four days on the plank with nobody's finger on it. Pike had said, to the page, when Cael asked why: "Pays on the map, at Oxhollow. That's a week north to get paid for a week's walk, and the crews that could take it are the crews with the pick. Nobody's been on the board long enough to want it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:10736:   207	*The traverse holds. So far. The map's good — Karis's, not mine; mine's the check. The herd's above the eighth narrowing tonight, thirty, grazing along. Karis says they go off declarations, as a unit, away, and on a slope away is down; which means the loudest thing on this mountain is us, and we've been quiet four days. Not one of us has had to try.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:10738:   209	*Nothing's drifted in four days. I'm writing that as a fact and not as a meaning. First honest job I've ever had that didn't have a fight at the end of it. I'm writing that before it's finished, which I don't do. Note that I did.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:10783:     5	He counted them from the root of the ledge in the grey before the sun, because counting was the first thing he did with anything, and got thirty. Thirty backs on the upper shelf, two hundred meters up by the eye, along the last of the green above the bench. At dusk they had been half a day behind. Now they were above, and moving: not the drift of four days but a walk, heads down, the line going along the shelf at a man's pace toward the bulge, where the upper shelf pinched out against the mountain as the lower did. The green ended there. The grinding came down to the bench and did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:10923:   145	Seln had it. He had asked at Thornwater about the thing the steading's woman had said: *the nearest walks a route and she's not due.* Oxhollow had no healer of its own. The nearest walked a circuit of the holds and was wherever on it she was. A man at Oxhollow in two days could send a carter along the void road to find her, and that was four days from any place on this mountain in any direction, counted generously.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:11078:    27	She walked like a carter: the pace set once at the start of a road and not changed for a slope or a narrow or anything short of an animal in the road; the feet going down whole and staying down; the weight never once ahead of them. Lira moved as if the ground were a thing she could leave. Oryn moved as if it were a thing she had an arrangement with. She was never fast. In four days he did not see her hurry, and he was watching for it — at the mule's stumbles, at the place on the second day where the ground went out from under Brom's box — and she came to each at the same walk, and was there when she was needed and not before. Item. She walked on the down-slope side of the mule, where a rider would come off. Item. She looked at the four of them who could walk as Seln looked at a road, every halt, once, all together, and then at whichever one had changed. Item. She had a heel gone raw in her boot from the mountain, and at the first halt she took the boot off and washed it and taped it with the same grey cloth she had put round his ribs, and put the boot back on, and walked. She did not put her hands on it and go still. She could not do that to herself. She had said so on the bench, and here it was, in a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:11094:    43	At the same halt Oryn came round the stone to him and said "Calf," and he gave it to her: Seln's binding, four days old and stiff with the mountain, and the wound under it, the ford's two mouths opened along the shale and closed again, mostly, as things closed on him. She looked at it as a carter looks at a wheel. She washed it from her skin and bound it fresh in her cloth, tight, and did not put her hands on it and go still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:11218:   163	She loaded it herself, at the gate, with the sun going off the bench above the roofs. The roll under the strap; the panniers, which had carried a box for four days and now carried the kit. Lira, on two feet with a hand on the stockade, watched her and said nothing; Brom held the mule's lead while she tightened a girth and gave it back without being asked, and she said "Hm," to him, and it was thanks. It was not a leaving that had been arranged. It was a healer at the end of a stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/plan-audit.stdout.log:11224:   169	He had a sentence. He had had it since the second night's fire, and it was a good sentence, and it asked her to walk south with five people instead of north with a mule, and he did not say it. He catalogued not saying it; that was the whole of what he did with it. She had seven holds and a child she did not know about. He had a board. She had told him at a fire what she owned, and it was not a thing you asked a person to set down for a crew that had known her four days.
