editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-24.md:51:"Who they ought to have. One's a bull wants bringing down off the top grazing before the snow shuts it, and he's killed a dog. The other's a ford that wants staking. They'd each of them heard a line said in this meadow about a river and five dead hounds." He blotted something. "I told them you were six days off on a hill. They said they'd wait a fortnight. After that they'll have Teague."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-40.md:285:"It's been off the tree longer than any apple on that shelf. That brown you're so fond of came down a fortnight after it, and it'll be meal before the snow's a week old." He had turned, and was reaching up to a shelf over the hearth, into the dark behind a row of crocks; and what he brought down was green, and long, and wrinkled like an old woman's knuckle. "That's the same tree as your sour one, and it's last year's. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-27.md:33:"It ends here," she said. "At about this fence, or an hour beyond; the plate isn't fine enough to say. I have carried this sheet for ninety-four days in case it should be of use, and from this gate on it is a clean piece of paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-23.md:7:It was the start of something, and so he took the inventory, as he took it at the start of anything. Three ribs were broken on the left side, low; they had been taped on the eighty-first day, and the tape was to come off on the ninety-ninth and not before. The calf was bound in a healer's cloth and was closing by itself, as his hurts did. Lira had a shin that was four days old, and an ankle that no healer on this road was going to mend, and one boot. Brom's left wrist wore a linen cuff, and he was to hang nothing from it until the middle of the eighty-seventh day. Karis had been told to ask nothing of herself for three days more, and she was asleep against a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-23.md:231:"Tomorrow is the ninetieth," she said. "The plank, and a smith. I have a slip in my coat with a horned head on it, and I want to hear what he does with a job that ended in a no." She turned a leaf. "And Thornwater on the ninety-third. That's four days, and two of them are road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-55.md:289:"Then here's the last of what I've got, and it's a healer's and not a theorist's. Write it down and stop touching it. Things you keep touching don't heal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-42.md:119:"There's a child. You told us about a child with a chest. I can keep it still for four days; I've kept it still for ten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:9:Lira was at her chalk at the south end with her back to the door. She had taped her own wrists but not the ankle, because the healer had said there was nothing left in it to tape, and she stood now with her weight even on both feet and her left arm tucked a little toward her ribs, from the old habit, and did not look round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-43.md:163:She had started it in the dairy week. Not that night; the night after, when she could not sleep for thinking about three carters and a road with four days of stamps on it and then none. She had thought then, lying on her back in the dark, about what she actually owned. Not the program. The program was Rooke's, and the east hall was the house's, and the red book belonged to a river academy and was only lent. The figure on the steward's sheet was hers, but it was hers the way a room is yours when somebody else holds the key; it meant something because a hall's seal was on it, and it would go on meaning something exactly as far as that seal was honoured.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-46.md:59:"He's just told you what it is. It's got a ruled edge and a word for itself. I don't need it read to me a third time." Her right hand had closed on the knot. "There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts. There's a fence at forty, the day the hold comes off it. That's ninety marks of work that was posted by people who want a fence. We'd be down from both before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-52.md:291:There was one thing more, and she had kept it for the last of the oil. She had been over the Fallow's eleven strips for four days at a trestle in a barn, and she had every stroke of them by heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-52.md:295:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-48.md:167:One by one they came down and tried it, since no one of them would take another's word for such a thing: Lira, and Brom, and Seln, each a stride past the stone and a stride back. The healer went over last, with two fingers on her own wrist and her lips moving, and came out again and nodded. Cael walked across beside Brom, and felt what he had felt on the Fallow, which was grass under his boots.
