editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r3.stdout.log:2566:"Three times." Lira sat, at last, on the end of the bench. "Three days, three carters, three different questions, so none of them would know they'd been asked anything." She held up her fingers one at a time. "The last Arbiter station on the east road is four days past Ostrand with a loaded wagon. Three with a light one. Every station stamps your sheet. After the last one, nobody stamps anything, and that's the Line. That's all it is. There's no post and no fence. It's the place the stamps stop." She lowered her hand. "Two of the three go past it every trip and make money doing it. Neither knows the other. They told me the same thing anyway. Past the stamps, nobody keeps a book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r3.stdout.log:3105:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:71:Brom had come up behind them, slower. At the turn of the stair he stopped, and Cael, a step below, saw his right hand go to the left side of his coat, where the inside pocket was, and rest there flat over the cloth. There was a square cream envelope in that pocket with its dark red seal still whole. It had come up with the post four days ago, and he had turned it over once at the table and put it away unopened, and nobody had asked. Now an office in his own province kept an open book with a question in it about his family, and his grandmother could turn its pages whenever she liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r3.stdout.log:3332:*The brass came up from the coast at last, and it is a third dearer than last year and not as good, and the founder says it is nobody's fault, which I believe, because it never is. I have a surveyor's level on the bench that came back to me after thirty years. I made it for the man's father. The bubble has wandered a hair, and the son brought it in to be put right; and then, when I had it in pieces, he asked me to set it back the way it was, because his father laid out every boundary in the valley with it a hair wrong and the whole valley is built to agree with it now. I spent four days deciding. In the end I did as he asked.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He let it stand. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-43.md:111:"No. You have not, in four days, and I had noticed." She took her hand from the lid. "It is the kindest thing that anybody has done for me since I crossed the Line, and I have entered it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-43.md:127:Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-43.md:205:"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-02.md:113:There were eight more. One wanted a night-watch kept on the landing, and one wanted stones picked out of a field, and one was a message to be walked to a hold four days off, for a mark and feed. Most were in the same square black hand, as if one person had written them out for others; but beside the words on each somebody had drawn a little picture, a wheel or a fence or a boat with sticks standing in it, for the use of people who could make nothing of the words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/completion/pre-lanes/chapter-26.md:25:"I had the last one read in a quarter of an hour, three times over, and then there was nothing left of it." He turned the red-ruled square in his fingers. "I've had one letter in ninety days. Now I've two, and four days of road in front of me. I'm going to have one of them at the wash tomorrow, at the noon halt. I'll keep the other for a noon with nothing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-46.md:283:He knew what would be done with it. The code let a seat proceed upon its own authority where a certificate was withheld; he had cited that section himself, once, long ago, when a guild hall's assessor would not sign. His line would stop nothing. The sittings would sit, and the ruling would be read, and somewhere in a minute a clerk would enter that a thing usually present was not there. That was the whole of what he could do, and he had weighed it on a post road for four days and found that it was still worth doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:2372:*The brass came up from the coast at last, and it is a third dearer than last year and not as good, and the founder says it is nobody's fault, which I believe, because it never is. I have a surveyor's level on the bench that came back to me after thirty years. I made it for the man's father. The bubble has wandered a hair, and the son brought it in to be put right; and then, when I had it in pieces, he asked me to set it back the way it was, because his father laid out every boundary in the valley with it a hair wrong and the whole valley is built to agree with it now. I spent four days deciding. In the end I did as he asked. I am telling you this because you will have an opinion, and I should like to hear it before I change my mind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/pre-repair-r1/chapter-22.md:65:He lay still on the scrubbed wood and looked at the underside of a turf roof. Nine things lay in him as quiet as he had ever known them; he had held them for eighty-four days, and it had become very like breathing. He could not tell what her fingers were reaching for. He knew only that none of the nine had stirred, and that if one had, he would have felt her feel it.
