editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/source-tracking.stdout.log:4300:    71	*The brass came up from the coast at last, and it is a third dearer than last year and not as good, and the founder says it is nobody's fault, which I believe, because it never is. I have a surveyor's level on the bench that came back to me after thirty years. I made it for the man's father. The bubble has wandered a hair, and the son brought it in to be put right; and then, when I had it in pieces, he asked me to set it back the way it was, because his father laid out every boundary in the valley with it a hair wrong and the whole valley is built to agree with it now. I spent four days deciding. In the end I did as he asked. I am telling you this because you will have an opinion, and I should like to hear it before I change my mind.*
