editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:6331:- **Day 81 onward:** tape, explained ("It tells the ribs where the edge is"); the lip, thirty-one paces; **four days down** with her walk catalogued (carter's pace; the down-slope side of the mule; the raw heel taped, because she can't heal herself). **Field care taught** (Lira learns to tape her own ankle properly; Brom learns what a wrapped wrist allows). **The ankle refused** on the arithmetic: "That's a tape and a season." **The route told** at the second night's fire: seven, twenty days, thirty-odd rounds, late eleven times and now twelve, what each owes and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; "I remember who I've had my hands on… That's the book."; the city's list by tier; the want (protected).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:32:  (1) Oryn's route sum in ch22: he is whole on the 99th by her rule, her Thornwater stop ends on the 95th, she plans four days of work at Thornwater's table and adds one day for the reading. Movement 4's packet has her walk the Fallow road in those days. Is the ch22 plan consistent with Movement 4 and Movement 5's packets and the BOOK_MAP day table if she changes it at the gate on the 93rd? If not, give the exact fix (the author offers: cut the second half of the paragraph beginning "She took it out of the round", keeping "She put a day on the route.").
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1205:- **Oryn's sum (ch22:207–229), as it now stands on the page:** broken on the 78th; twenty-one days by the rule. Her hands say that he will be sound some days sooner, but she cannot read him, so she cannot know it, and she will not lift a tape on a guess: **whole on the 99th**. The long reading wants him whole and still and wants most of a day of her: **the 100th is the first morning she can run it.** She is at Thornwater on the 93rd (he is fifteen days from the break); her stop ends on the 95th. **"A week after she had gone" is now "four days after she had gone"**, and the round would not bring her back for twenty days. From the 95th to the 100th is five days. Four of them she can spend at Thornwater's own table as honest work, since the grain is moving and its bench will fill again; they make her late at six gates, and she knows whom she keeps waiting. The fifth, the day of the reading, mends nobody on her route and is owed by no hold: "If it was to be had, it must be added." **She put a day on the route.** The paragraphs on wanting are unchanged and now stand between the sum and the decision.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1206:- This agrees with the day table (Thornwater 93; tape off 99; the reading on the 100th) and with the later lateness count (nine days late at Thornwater on the 104th means a stop that should have ended on the 95th). **For the coordinator:** Movement 4 has her walk the Fallow road with the crew on the 94th–98th instead of sitting at Thornwater's table. Her plan here is what she intends on the 92nd; she changes it at the gate on the 93rd ("You're a stop. I've added you."). If the ledger would sooner have her plan nothing for the four days, the paragraph beginning "She took it out of the round" can lose its second half without touching the last line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1240:- **E, the four days.** I closed the sum by having her plan to work four days at Thornwater's table and add one, rather than add five or change "a day". That makes the page add up and keeps the packet's sentence; it also gives her a plan which Movement 4 overturns at the gate. Flagged above.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1247:- **Oryn's sum:** whole on the 99th by the rule; the reading on the 100th at the earliest; her stop at Thornwater ends on the 95th; four days of work there and one day put on the route.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1304:| Days 81–84: tape and lip on 81; second night on 82; valley on 83; Oxhollow midmorning 84 (`manuscript/chapter-21.md:27,49-51,117-159,215-223,301-303`) | Correct; “four days down” is inclusive 81–84. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1503:- **Day 81 onward:** tape, explained ("It tells the ribs where the edge is"); the lip, thirty-one paces; **four days down** with her walk catalogued (carter's pace; the down-slope side of the mule; the raw heel taped, because she can't heal herself). **Field care taught** (Lira learns to tape her own ankle properly; Brom learns what a wrapped wrist allows). **The ankle refused** on the arithmetic: "That's a tape and a season." **The route told** at the second night's fire: seven, twenty days, thirty-odd rounds, late eleven times and now twelve, what each owes and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; "I remember who I've had my hands on… That's the book."; the city's list by tier; the want (protected).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1615:- **Two days back (98–99):** Lira walking flat-footed as she is told; the calf closing with no count on the flat by the second noon. **Thornwater:** "Six. Well." Tape off in the yard ("Twenty-one days. A rib is six weeks. These are done."). The calf closed in four days. "Still and whole." / "You don't get a yes. You get told." / "What does helping look like?" / "I'll know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1709:- **Oryn's sum (ch22:207–229), as it now stands on the page:** broken on the 78th; twenty-one days by the rule. Her hands say that he will be sound some days sooner, but she cannot read him, so she cannot know it, and she will not lift a tape on a guess: **whole on the 99th**. The long reading wants him whole and still and wants most of a day of her: **the 100th is the first morning she can run it.** She is at Thornwater on the 93rd (he is fifteen days from the break); her stop ends on the 95th. **"A week after she had gone" is now "four days after she had gone"**, and the round would not bring her back for twenty days. From the 95th to the 100th is five days. Four of them she can spend at Thornwater's own table as honest work, since the grain is moving and its bench will fill again; they make her late at six gates, and she knows whom she keeps waiting. The fifth, the day of the reading, mends nobody on her route and is owed by no hold: "If it was to be had, it must be added." **She put a day on the route.** The paragraphs on wanting are unchanged and now stand between the sum and the decision.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1710:- This agrees with the day table (Thornwater 93; tape off 99; the reading on the 100th) and with the later lateness count (nine days late at Thornwater on the 104th means a stop that should have ended on the 95th). **For the coordinator:** Movement 4 has her walk the Fallow road with the crew on the 94th–98th instead of sitting at Thornwater's table. Her plan here is what she intends on the 92nd; she changes it at the gate on the 93rd ("You're a stop. I've added you."). If the ledger would sooner have her plan nothing for the four days, the paragraph beginning "She took it out of the round" can lose its second half without touching the last line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:1744:- **E, the four days.** I closed the sum by having her plan to work four days at Thornwater's table and add one, rather than add five or change "a day". That makes the page add up and keeps the packet's sentence; it also gives her a plan which Movement 4 overturns at the gate. Flagged above.
