editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7736:Teague's crew was gone on the hold contracts, a week out and a week back, and Oxhollow's slip had come in on a carter behind them and sat four days on the plank with nobody's finger on it. Pike had said, to the page, when Cael asked why: "Pays on the map, at Oxhollow. That's a week north to get paid for a week's walk, and the crews that could take it are the crews with the pick. Nobody's been on the board long enough to want it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7928:*The traverse holds. So far. The map's good — Karis's, not mine; mine's the check. The herd's above the eighth narrowing tonight, thirty, grazing along. Karis says they go off declarations, as a unit, away, and on a slope away is down; which means the loudest thing on this mountain is us, and we've been quiet four days. Not one of us has had to try.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7930:*Nothing's drifted in four days. I'm writing that as a fact and not as a meaning. First honest job I've ever had that didn't have a fight at the end of it. I'm writing that before it's finished, which I don't do. Note that I did.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7975:He counted them from the root of the ledge in the grey before the sun, because counting was the first thing he did with anything, and got thirty. Thirty backs on the upper shelf, two hundred meters up by the eye, along the last of the green above the bench. At dusk they had been half a day behind. Now they were above, and moving: not the drift of four days but a walk, heads down, the line going along the shelf at a man's pace toward the bulge, where the upper shelf pinched out against the mountain as the lower did. The green ended there. The grinding came down to the bench and did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8115:Seln had it. He had asked at Thornwater about the thing the steading's woman had said: *the nearest walks a route and she's not due.* Oxhollow had no healer of its own. The nearest walked a circuit of the holds and was wherever on it she was. A man at Oxhollow in two days could send a carter along the void road to find her, and that was four days from any place on this mountain in any direction, counted generously.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8266:She walked like a carter: the pace set once at the start of a road and not changed for a slope or a narrow or anything short of an animal in the road; the feet going down whole and staying down; the weight never once ahead of them. Lira moved as if the ground were a thing she could leave. Oryn moved as if it were a thing she had an arrangement with. She was never fast. In four days he did not see her hurry, and he was watching for it — at the mule's stumbles, at the place on the second day where the ground went out from under Brom's box — and she came to each at the same walk, and was there when she was needed and not before. Item. She walked on the down-slope side of the mule, where a rider would come off. Item. She looked at the four of them who could walk as Seln looked at a road, every halt, once, all together, and then at whichever one had changed. Item. She had a heel gone raw in her boot from the mountain, and at the first halt she took the boot off and washed it and taped it with the same grey cloth she had put round his ribs, and put the boot back on, and walked. She did not put her hands on it and go still. She could not do that to herself. She had said so on the bench, and here it was, in a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8282:At the same halt Oryn came round the stone to him and said "Calf," and he gave it to her: Seln's binding, four days old and stiff with the mountain, and the wound under it, the ford's two mouths opened along the shale and closed again, mostly, as things closed on him. She looked at it as a carter looks at a wheel. She washed it from her skin and bound it fresh in her cloth, tight, and did not put her hands on it and go still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8402:She loaded it herself, at the gate, with the sun going off the bench above the roofs. The roll under the strap; the panniers, which had carried a box for four days and now carried the kit. Lira, on two feet with a hand on the stockade, watched her and said nothing; Brom held the mule's lead while she tightened a girth and gave it back without being asked, and she said "Hm," to him, and it was thanks. It was not a leaving that had been arranged. It was a healer at the end of a stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8408:He had a sentence. He had had it since the second night's fire, and it was a good sentence, and it asked her to walk south with five people instead of north with a mule, and he did not say it. He catalogued not saying it; that was the whole of what he did with it. She had seven holds and a child she did not know about. He had a board. She had told him at a fire what she owned, and it was not a thing you asked a person to set down for a crew that had known her four days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:8834:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:213:So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:9849:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:213:So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:9931:| Days 81–84: tape and lip on 81; second night on 82; valley on 83; Oxhollow midmorning 84 (`manuscript/chapter-21.md:27,49-51,117-159,215-223,301-303`) | Correct; “four days down” is inclusive 81–84. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:10055:- **Day 81 onward:** tape, explained ("It tells the ribs where the edge is"); the lip, thirty-one paces; **four days down** with her walk catalogued (carter's pace; the down-slope side of the mule; the raw heel taped, because she can't heal herself). **Field care taught** (Lira learns to tape her own ankle properly; Brom learns what a wrapped wrist allows). **The ankle refused** on the arithmetic: "That's a tape and a season." **The route told** at the second night's fire: seven, twenty days, thirty-odd rounds, late eleven times and now twelve, what each owes and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; "I remember who I've had my hands on… That's the book."; the city's list by tier; the want (protected).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:10105:So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:10171:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md-73-| 18 | Tape; the lip; four days down; the route told (seven holds, twenty days); "The route is the only thing I own."; Oxhollow's table; the map paid; three readings; the two readings taught; three questions; "Thornwater's on my route in nine days…"; Teague at the ford: "The bench was a cart." (Ch7) | 81–84 | KEEP |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:10187:editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/BOOK_MAP.md-139-**Two hard rules for every window:** (1) **No member of Teague's crew is named, ever**, including in Teague's own head. Book 8 kills "the young one" at the pass and says his name once without entering it. Teague thinks of his people as the spear, the bow, the boy and the healer, which is how a captain counts. (2) **Oryn's holds stay unnamed** except Oxhollow, Thornwater and Millrace. In her head they are what they owe her or what is there: the hold with the child; the hold that gave the coat; the fourth, four days on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:10293:27:- **Two days back (98–99):** Lira walking flat-footed as she is told; the calf closing with no count on the flat by the second noon. **Thornwater:** "Six. Well." Tape off in the yard ("Twenty-one days. A rib is six weeks. These are done."). The calf closed in four days. "Still and whole." / "You don't get a yes. You get told." / "What does helping look like?" / "I'll know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:10307:31:- **Cael:** **nineteen** (turned on the page on the 102nd, at Thornwater). **Ten confirmed fragments.** The tenth is **Tide-adjacent** (Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; *Engagement: clinical — non-hostile*), acquired on the 100th. The anomaly is now **a location, not an entry: the still place.** It was read three times. It does not flow and does not change, and everything goes around it. It is not the Quiet and not a fragment. Not touched since a healer said *stop*. Ribs quiet again since the marsh. Calf a closed seam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:10447:325-+So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
