editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3230:-He went to the first sitting's minute, which was still in the grey-taped bundle, and laid it beside the denial. Five days ago, at the wool exchange, the five chairs of the evaluation seat had sat under grey covers, and the minute said *to be called*. This morning the same seat was *fully constituted*. Somewhere in the four days between that sitting and this refusal, then, somebody had filled five chairs. He knew every senior evaluator in that district by name and grade. Not one of them had written to him for the boy's file, which any of them would have needed before they sat; and none of the five chairs had been offered to the one officer who had already sat across a table from the boy with an instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3237:+*Minute of the 40th: evaluation seat, to be called. Refusal of the 44th: fully constituted. Who sat down in those five chairs in four days, without asking this office for the file?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4086: Brom had come up behind them, slower. At the turn of the stair he stopped, and Cael, a step below, saw his right hand go to the left side of his coat, where the inside pocket was, and rest there flat over the cloth. There was a square cream envelope in that pocket with its dark red seal still whole. It had come up with the post four days ago, and he had turned it over once at the table and put it away unopened, and nobody had asked. Now an office in his own province kept an open book with a question in it about his family, and his grandmother could turn its pages whenever she liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4117: *The brass came up from the coast at last, and it is a third dearer than last year and not as good, and the founder says it is nobody's fault, which I believe, because it never is. I have a surveyor's level on the bench that came back to me after thirty years. I made it for the man's father. The bubble has wandered a hair, and the son brought it in to be put right; and then, when I had it in pieces, he asked me to set it back the way it was, because his father laid out every boundary in the valley with it a hair wrong and the whole valley is built to agree with it now. I spent four days deciding. In the end I did as he asked.*
