editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:6114:books/book-04-copper-crown/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:41:**The campus:** A mid-tier academy on a river bluff above Ostrand, a trade city of real size — four days' travel from Greyvane, a different world entirely. Where Greyvane was a converted waystation with one training hall, Halcenvane is purpose-built: three training halls, a formal assessment wing, a lecture complex, residence quadrangles, a library that would swallow Prynn's archive whole, and the Crown yard — a tiered public bout floor at the campus's center, because at Halcenvane competition is not an extracurricular. It is the institution's circulatory system. Mid-tier means: legitimate, chartered, competitive, ambitious — big enough that the Compact inspects it on a schedule, small enough that it still has something to prove. Halcenvane's identity is the opposite of Greyvane's in one precise way: Greyvane took students the system mispriced out of conviction. Halcenvane took Cael as an *investment*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:6214:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:24:- **What Leaving Costs**: three roads priced; the cache argued for and lost out loud; **Bracken brings Brom's finding at night** — Iron, found, and the guidance note — "How many boxes is your research?" / "Three." / "Then I'm carrying three boxes."; Lira's whole bill; Karis's forfeiture priced in bibliography order (the Greyvane request redirected to Hesk; the assistant's letter); Seln's one sentence; **Ephram brought in, told the departure and nothing of the cache, asks for one bout**; five hands, Seln's last. **Withrow** alone: the four days she knew and said nothing; the other column; **"The record will say you left…"**; she opens the door herself. **Lira's window** (~1.5k) that night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:6304:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-001/compiled-prompt.md:521:It was dated four days before. Hesk had not had his six pages. He could not have; they were still somewhere on the hill road, in Bracken's pouch. He had written off the broadsides, which reached the coast faster than any pouch. Then he had worked out which road the wagons would take, and which crossroads they would come to on which day, and sent the letter to meet them there. He had been right about all of it. Hesk's answers had always set out before the questions reached him.
