editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:They did not go back as they had come. Seln had read the hide by Karis's lamp on the crest, and had found them a shorter way than a marsh and a stair: along the ridge to the heap of chock-stones, and then north by the hide carters' road, which ran through shut holds toward a river that all of them knew. He walked it ahead of them, as he walked everything. Four times in a day he came back along the file and said his word, and after the second day Karis left off writing down the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:81:He had not meant to keep it so. He had put it into his coat in a cold room because it was registry paper. Reading registry paper was the one craft that he had brought east with him. That had been true. It was also true that he had read it to nobody since. A researcher had wanted one figure out of it, at a fire by a marsh, and had not felt able to ask. He had gone to her. He had held the page to the light for her three times with his fingers shut on its spine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:151:"I've a knife for cheese." He turned the bill over. "So have you. I've looked at all five of us. There's the one long knife, and she chose it for how it sat on two fingers. Karis has a thing with a horn handle that she sharpens pencils on." He lowered his voice, though the fifth of them was thirty paces ahead on the verge. "And he's got one I've never seen, and I'd bet the forty that it's the best of the lot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:185:He had crossed the ford three times and never known that it was called anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:261:Lira ran the bar. She did it without being told, in her bare feet first and then in her boots, from the blunt head to the point of the tail, flat out, three times; and each time she stopped dead on the last yard of gravel, on both feet, with the deep water in front of her and on either hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:281:There was nothing to do it against. He lay on his back in scrub that smelled of dust, with the sun climbing to the top of the sky, and the fragments offered him things. Anchor had found the lip of the ledge from the first moment he saw it, and it asked about that edge now, patiently, though the edge was three hundred yards behind him. *There?* He told it that the stones were the hold's, and to leave them. Storm lay in him like a full pail and showed him the air above the river, cool and heavy and sliding downstream, as a lane half made already. Wind wanted the bar. It had watched Lira run that gravel three times and it had her stride by heart, and it leaned in his feet though they were pointing at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:99:The boxes went first, passed across the crack from two pairs of hands to two more. He had the cart emptied to her boards. He folded his coat into a pad and laid it on the back of his neck, and he had Seln and Cael step over the gap and take a shaft each from the far side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:9:Seln was already over. He walked as he walked every approach, without haste, with his hands a little away from his sides and the strap of a locked case across his chest. When he had gone ten yards he lifted two fingers, and Lira stepped over the stone after him, on the whole sole of the great boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:247:Then she became somebody else, as Cael had seen her do once before, at the rim of a white floor, with a strip of her own shirt in her teeth. She took the lamp from Karis and held it close, and looked at the wound from in front and from behind, for a long time, without touching it. She laid two fingers on the inside of Brom's left wrist and counted, with her lips, to a good deal more than ten, and put her ear to his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:31:This was a different thing altogether. A stride beyond that stone she was three parts full; a healer had told her so with two fingers on her wrist. The till was heavy. There was no clerk, and no counter, and no room for them to stand in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:115:It stood a boot's width from the place where she had stopped, and the grass had hidden it. When Brom parted the stems with the side of his hand they all saw it: a square bar of iron, as thick as two fingers, driven straight down into the turf so that a hand's length of it stood clear. It was brown with rust and flaking, and its head had been beaten a little flat, and spread at the edges, by something heavy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:3:They had not gone to their blankets, and they had not gone down to the yard. They were sitting among the barley sacks where he had left them the night before, in very nearly the same places, with a shutter propped open for the light and two torn leaves still lying on the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:55:Brom got up off the boards, with the air of a man who has sat through a sermon and has an errand afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:95:"That's what I thought. You've been lying there with your eyes open like a man in a room full of talk." Brom shifted over on the boards to make a place. "Loud, is it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:115:"I'd not know. I'm telling you about a shed." Brom got his legs up onto the boards. "But I'd leave off counting them, for a start. You know that there are nine, and you knew it yesterday. They'll still be nine when you wake."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:295:He slept that night for four hours together, which was four more than the night before. The nine were all there when he woke in the dark, each in its place, with a wall of barley standing over them; and he let them be nine without going along the row. A forge was a forge. The low sound lay where it had lain down at noon, and the boards under his back came to him almost when they should.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:65:They waited, and the big brown wrists stayed the colour of wrists. He turned them over to look at the insides, and turned them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:191:He held it out. She took it between both of hers, as she had taken a carter's palm on a plank at Thornwater, with two fingers of her right hand laid along the inside where the pulse was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:9:She did not make a matter of it. She went to Brom first, as she went to the worst on any bench, and looked at four yards of webbing from her stride and a half, and then came the rest of the way and put two fingers under the lowest turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:She shifted the rope on her shoulder. She was looking up at the notch, and her face was as tired and as level as he had ever seen it; and whatever had given under her at a marsh's edge, three days before, had been put back where she kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:111:It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Three floors lay in a row, as stones lie along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:167:He had seen four such stakes. There had been one at a grey stone on the Fallow, and two at the Stair with a road between, and one at the track's end beside a marsh. Each had stood alone at an edge where a cord had been laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:203:"There was a sheet with two," he said, after a breath. "I told you that I could not place it. A marsh three days behind us, and this: I can place it now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:141:Seln considered her, and then, with perfect gravity, he lifted his own right hand and shook it from the wrist, three times, with the fingers spread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:159:"I ran at that," said Lira. Her voice had gone thin. "I ran at it three times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:7:She turned it edgewise. It was as thick as two fingers in the middle and thinned toward the rim, and it was built in leaves, one upon another, like the hoof of a horse or the bark of the red trees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:83:It shut on him three times before the top, and each time it let go a little sooner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:175:"One. Twenty-six feet. A hand and two fingers in the yard, outward. Slab; hollow at fifty on the outer third. No water. Cart: yes, keeping in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:157:"I've read him three times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:163:She came over all the same, on her knees, since she had been asked plainly. She took his right wrist, which had nothing wrong with it but the cold, and laid two fingers on the side of his neck, and her lips began to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:"I had the last one read in a quarter of an hour, three times over, and then there was nothing left of it." He turned the red-ruled square in his fingers. "I've had one letter in ninety days. Now I've two, and four days of road in front of me. I'm going to have one of them at the wash tomorrow, at the noon halt. I'll keep the other for a noon with nothing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:159:On the platform the bowman lifted two fingers from his stave. Lira put up the great boot, an inch, and set it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:255:Oryn had two fingers on her wrist already, and two on her neck, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:27:The bowman had come along the fence to that gate. It was not his; he kept the western one, and Cael had never seen him ten yards from his platform. He stood with his bow at the length of his arm and looked at the road going up the valley, and then at Lira, and he lifted two fingers from the stave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:133:He gave it, and she laid two fingers on the inside of it and two on his neck, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:17:He looked for the wall first, since every town he had ever entered had kept one, even Denvash, which was nothing; and a wall meant a gate, and a gate meant a man in it with a book. He looked for the station next, the low stone house with the windowless building behind, and for a board of hours, and for any roof with a seal burned into its sign. His eye went over the place three times in search of them, like a tongue going back to where a tooth has been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:147:The left sleeve of his coat was folded and pinned at the elbow, neatly, with a brass pin gone dull. Under the right arm he carried a tall book in boards, and he had a pen behind his ear. He was broad and not tall, with a grey stubble and a brown bald head, and his eyes were the colour of the tar on the plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:167:One by one they came down and tried it, since no one of them would take another's word for such a thing: Lira, and Brom, and Seln, each a stride past the stone and a stride back. The healer went over last, with two fingers on her own wrist and her lips moving, and came out again and nodded. Cael walked across beside Brom, and felt what he had felt on the Fallow, which was grass under his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:It was the same pattern as the one that Cael had knelt by on the ninety-sixth day, a cord north of a grey stone: a square bar as thick as two fingers, with its head beaten down by a mallet. This one was scaled all over with rust, in leaves, so that it looked more like a thing grown than a thing made. A thumb's breadth under the head there was the registry's stamp, and under the stamp, punched deep, were two figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:17:He was on the boards with his knees up, and his hands lay open on them, one of the two pale across the pads of the fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:21:It was out, and it lay on the boards among them. He went on, since he had built the rest with some care and wished them to see every joint of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:45:Karis wrote for the length of three breaths. Then she did a thing that he had never seen her do to that book: she tore the leaf out along its stitching, carefully, and laid it on the boards in front of the lantern, facing the rest of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:121:He had not shifted from his place on the boards. He was looking at his own open hand, where a healer had drawn four new lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:181:It lay on the boards beside the two torn leaves. He had nothing to put against it, since there was nothing that could be put.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:197:"You could have done it and told us after," she said. "You could have told nobody at all. You carried it up a ladder and let four people call it what it is." She touched the top of his head once, with two fingers, as she might have touched a card for luck. "It's still wrong. Go and write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:231:She had run that gravel three times yesterday, and the dog had never run it at all with anybody after it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:259:She was two strides from the edge with all that speed in her, and she did what she had spent a month on a river bar failing to do, and one morning under a platform doing at last while a man lifted two fingers from a bow. She stopped. She did it between one stride and the next, dead, on both feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:Cael watched the carter at that halt, and at the next, and learned more from his hands than he had from his mouth. The man carried a lump of mutton fat in a rag, and he worked it into both axles every time the cart stood, though neither had begun to complain. His trace chains were bound in strips of old sacking from end to end, so that they could not ring. He had cut the iron heel-plates off his own boots, a long time ago by the look of the leather, and when the mule threw up its head at a bird he laid two fingers on its nose before it could think of making a remark. He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say; but everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:225:So, first: it kept its station, and he checked that three times. The ruts bent a little to the right, toward the swell, and the grey shape gave ground, drifting up the slope until there were forty-five yards between them again. The ruts bent left, and it came down. Whatever it wanted, it wanted it from exactly that far away, like a watcher who has been told his distance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Pike did not wait for her to finish. Another slip lay ready under the edge of his stone, where somebody had left it for him earlier in the day. He drew it out with two fingers, and read it to himself, and read it again, and he was giving it to the meadow before the last of their silver was off the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:181:It was a low room, longer than it was wide, with beams gone black and a hearth at the far end that a man could have stood in. Benches ran down both sides, with thirty or forty people on them, by crews, each crew at its own boards, and the noise dropped a little as the five came through the door and then rose again. He was looked at once, and not twice, by people who had heard a line said about him two hours ago and wanted nothing more just yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:227:Brom had not come over, and sat at their own boards with the jug in front of him, looking up the wall with his lips moving. He started at the bottom and went to the top. Then he did it again, from the top down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:235:She put it on the bench, a full arm's length from the table, and she wiped the boards with her sleeve; then she laid the calfskin out between them and held its corners down, and all five of them leaned over it. It was the first time Cael had been able to look at it with a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:257:Nobody at that table spoke of it again, that night or for a long time after. Seln lifted his hand from the edge of the hide. Then he took the skin by two of its corners and turned it on the boards, a half circle, so that the ring with the bar through it lay nearest the door, and everything else that a carter had drawn in twenty-two years ran away from them toward the hearth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:327:They were at the boards nearest the door, on his right, with a lamp and one jug. He took them as he took ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:343:There was a man at the end of the bench. He sat a little back from the lamp, in a dark coat, with a strap across his chest and a cup in both hands, and his eyes were shut. Teague's look had gone across him the first time as it would have gone across a patch of plaster. He was the same height sitting as everybody else, and the same colour as the wall. He had chosen the one place at those boards that the light from the door did not quite reach, and Teague did not believe for a moment that he had chosen it by chance, or that he was asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:39:Lira woke as she did everything. He had put two fingers on her shoulder, and she was sitting up with her hand shut on his wrist before he had taken them away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:47:"I counted three times, and there's nobody." He heard how it sounded. "I don't know what to do with the end of the count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:111:Brom had the gelding out of the shafts. He stood over the ring a while with the hobbles in his hand; then he crouched, and turned one of the stones over with two fingers, and turned it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:135:Brom made no sound that could be heard at ten yards. His boots moved on the turf. Her two hands were round the shoulder before the wood was clear, one above the place and one below; her eyes shut; and Cael, who had sat all day in a stone room and heard her count that man's heart with two fingers, saw her lips reach three and stop counting, since she had what she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:93:The bowman had been watching her come. He was a lean, weathered man of no particular age, who seemed to live on that platform, and Cael had not yet heard him use his voice. He looked down at Lira standing under him with her hands on her knees. Then he lifted two fingers from the stave of his bow, an inch, and laid them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:163:He had also brought a scrap of bark. He took it out of his hat and held it out to Cael between two fingers, as though he were not sure that he ought to be touching it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:15:It was the thing he had seen her do under a rock, and he understood it better for seeing it done eight times. She stopped in front of each of them at her stride and a half and looked. Then she put two fingers on a wrist, or a hand on the hurt place, and her lips moved to ten, and she passed on to the next without a word. An old man with a cough. A child on a lap. A woman who held her left forearm away from her body, wrapped in a cloth that had been wet and dried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:63:He gave it. She laid two fingers on the inside of it, and two of the other hand on the side of his neck below the jaw, as she had laid them on Karis, and this time she shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:"That's three times. The first I thought it was the break; a bad one can shout over everything. So I went where you're whole. There's nothing wrong with that wrist, or that neck, and there's still nothing I can read." She looked at Karis, who was the one writing it down, or would be. "It's like putting my hands in a river in the dark. Something's moving. I can't find the banks, so I can't read it, so I can't touch it. That's the rule. I didn't make it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:141:She had put them on that boy three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:191:She got there on the eighty-ninth evening, and she did not eat first. That was a rule broken, and she knew it, and she went straight through the yard to a house under a pear tree and put two fingers on a small wrist and two on a neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:223:On the morning of the ninety-second, on a level stretch with frost in the ruts, she did the thing she had done three times before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:331:*Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:39:"There it is. I've got the moving. I had that much at a ford with two fingers." Her hand had not shifted on his chest. "Now I go and look for what it's moving in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:87:Cael had not known that he ever did. He walked across the yard in his slow dry way with a length of ash in his hand, peeled, as tall as his shoulder, with a fork left at the top of it for a palm to sit in, and he stood it against the trough where Lira's hand would find it. He kept his eyes off her foot; he lifted two fingers from his side, an inch, and let them fall, and went back up his ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:231:Cael wrote it that evening in the loft door, with his leg stretched along the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:"Twice. It'll hold twice. It will not hold three times this season, so don't ask it to" — and Brom said, "Arm," and she said, "No," and he didn't.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:75:It was only three words. But it had not come out as her other sentences came, each one finished and set down like a bowl on a board; the middle of it had given a little under her, as a plate gives underfoot. He made no answer, since he did not think that she could have borne to be answered. She had told a mill that she would be nobody's sixth, and a board at Thornwater that she was a stop; she had eaten out of their bowl for a week, standing; and this was the only way that she had of saying what a person became to her when she had gone into a marsh after his friend and watched him go into worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:81:He lay down on the turf where he was. She came the stride and a half and got down on her knees, and he felt two fingers on the inside of his wrist and two under his jaw, as cold as the iron behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:171:Seln had the watch. He sat on the grey stone where Lira had sat, with his back to the fire, and whatever he heard behind him he gave no sign of hearing; the rest were four long shapes under blankets. Cael lay on his back between the embers and the old iron, and laid two fingers on the inside of his wrist and a palm on his breastbone, and let his breath go out until he had none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:243:He was in, with the single bed about him, and the one deep thing in it, running. It was the strangest place in which he had ever tried to tell the truth: he went along a healer's river at his own uneven pace, with her banks standing close on either hand, and said aloud to her what the same going-along had found three times in a body that had none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:245:"There are ten in me. You found more than one and couldn't count them. It's ten, and I've counted them three times. Not one has a side. I looked for an hour last night for a bank like these, and there isn't any."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:263:"I gave it one a long while back, on an old leaf, when it was only something that another person's hands had caught the edge of. The still place. I never had anything to lay the words on till last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:"A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:289:"Then here's the last of what I've got, and it's a healer's and not a theorist's. Write it down and stop touching it. Things you keep touching don't heal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:293:"I don't know what it is, and nor do you. I know what a person does with a place in himself that he's found: he goes back to it every night with his fingers." She was already turning toward Brom's blankets. "You've looked three times. You know where it is, and that it keeps. Let it keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:"Because I cannot mend what I can't read." The healer said it without heat. "I read her leg at a count of ten and I knew what it was, and how she was built round it, and where the thing that knits had stopped; and then I knew where to put mine. I had my hands on him for three times as long and I got bone. I'd have got that much with my eyes shut, off a dead sheep." She showed her palms, briefly. "Past the bone there's nothing I can lay hold of. I'd be pushing in the dark, and I don't do that to anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:83:Seln came back for Karis. He put two fingers through the back of her belt and walked behind her, and she went across with her lips moving; and when she reached the sound ledge she turned round and studied the scar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:9:They were the whole of the forenoon about that perimeter, with the marsh on their right and the frost going grey on the turf. Not once in that round did the water stand a yard inside the line, or a reed a yard beyond it. A spring had filled that basin for longer than anybody could say, and it had filled it to a line that five people could feel in their heads and no farther. Cael could not have said what water knew about such an edge, and not one of them tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:57:The two of them knelt, one on either side. He could not follow the whole of what they did. The shirt-tail came off, and a pad of raw wool went up under the arm. Then a strip of stout webbing as broad as a hand was carried round Brom's chest, and over the point of the shoulder, and back beneath the elbow, three times; and the lean woman laid that figure without once looking at her fingers. Oryn held where she was told to hold. Half way through she stopped the other's wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:137:He looked at the rope between the reeds. There were breeches on it, frozen as stiff as boards, six pairs, and they had not got so wet by walking on grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:165:The tenth did not stir, since it never asked for anything. But he knew what was within his reach, as a man with a key in his pocket knows a lock when he passes one. It would take two fingers, and a count of ten. At three he would have this man's banks under his hand, and at ten he would know the Path as he knew a friend's step. In a minute more he would have what ran in it, and how deep. That was the Bronze which a one-armed man had guessed at under his eave, and had never been told. A registrar would have given a year's pay for what lay a foot from his fingers. He could have had the whole of it and told nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:203:*Teague sat down next to me. Asked what I am. I could have read him. I asked instead. The day I stop asking is the day I'm the registry. Write it down. Again. Every time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:185:He saw the foot go over. It went outward, past the place where an ankle stops, as it had gone on round stones at a river and under a plate of shale; and that made three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:9:"It need not." Karis set the point of her pencil against the brass at its lower end, and made a dot on the skin; then she slid the strip down along itself with two fingers, a hand's length, until the dot and the lower ring both lay against it again. "A ruler is as long as its owner is patient."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:11:She did it three times. Cael watched the bright edge go down the hide, past the square beyond the ridge that was a mill, and out over the place where the carter's hill-hatching thinned. All six of them had their heads over the box by then, with the healer a stride and a half behind the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:They slept that night in a halting-ring, with a fire of Seln's flint, and Cael sat his watch with Hesk's book shut on his knee. He owed a healer two readings of himself yet, on two different days, and he had not laid a finger on his own wrist since she told him *not tonight* between two bars of iron. He left it so. She had asked for a night that was only a night; every one since had been the eve of a floor or the morrow of one, and he thought it likely that this was the eve of another. The debt weighed on him a good deal less than it had on a halting-ground above the wash, and he took that for a sign that he had begun to believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:101:Seln had stopped ahead of them, on the crown of a low rise, with his hands at his sides. He did not lift two fingers. He stood where he was until the five of them had come up beside him and could see what he saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:103:The ground fell away in front of them into a basin as round as a plate. It was full of reeds, the colour of old straw, with black water shining between the stems; and the reeds all stopped at one edge, on every side, as though somebody had gone round them with a pair of shears. From the foot of the rise the old ruts came out of the turf again and ran straight down to that edge. Beyond it something pale and narrow went on across the marsh in the same line, as straight as a ruled stroke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:133:She looked along the reeds to the right, and then to the left, as far as the marsh's curve would let her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:161:Cael had taken it for the ordinary margin of a marsh. It was black, and wet, and trodden by nothing; but all along the water's edge, a yard or two inside the reeds, it had been raised into long low ridges, each as broad as a cart's bed, like the runs that a mole throws up in a meadow. They went curving away on both hands with the curve of the basin. The reeds leaned off them to either side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:179:Lira had not been listening to either of them. She stood at the stone with her right hand over her eyes, and she was looking along the pale straight thing that crossed the marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:223:It was two strides broad, of the same grey, and it went away to the right and the left in a great curve; Cael put the whole round of it at seventy yards across. Within it there was water, a foot or two deep and as clear as a well's, moving slowly outward and spilling over the kerb's low places into the marsh. Under the water lay the floor. It had been one floor once. It was broken now into great plates, like the ice on a pond that has gone to pieces and frozen again, and every broken edge was round. Thirty yards off, in the middle of it, the walls stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:231:She timed it. The knocking went away round the dish to the left, behind the hall, and was lost; and when the hand of the clock had gone a quarter of the way round its face, it came back from the right, plate after plate, and passed below their boots. She let it go round three times before she would write the figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:233:"Once in a quarter of an hour, and I have it three times," she said. "Brom. The largest that you brought."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:383:*Hundred and eighteenth day. The edge of the third one, at the track's end. A marsh, and the reeds stop where the sigils stop. One bar, thirty-three, old, and no second. A hall in the middle of it, and a thing under the hall's floor that a carter told the healer nobody has seen whole.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:83:She had two fingers on his wrist again. She could not read him; she could count his heart as any woman in a steading could, and she did it every quarter of an hour by some clock that she carried in her head, and said the same one word after it, which was *there*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:95:"I am touching it. That is a different thing, and I had better be honest about which." Her voice was small and exact in the dark. "It is cut as the bands on those stones were cut. I can find a hooked stroke with two bars across its stem under my second finger, and I cannot tell by touch whether the bars stand close or wide; so I shall not say that it is the same hand." She let her hand come down. "I have a wall here that I would give a year of my life to read. It is against my spine. My paper is in a box at a stone, and there is a finger of oil in that lamp, and the oil is for his shoulder if it turns. I have gone over that three times, to see whether I could make it come out otherwise. It will not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:149:He lifted two fingers from the goatskin, an inch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:He had been written on a good many boards. A woman in Ardenmere had chalked him up with a question mark, and a panel had posted him at a rate that it had scattered on purpose, and for two years a house had put his name on a card every week because a schedule told it to. Nobody had ever before gone to the keeper of a book and asked who was good, and been given him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:65:It had come down a mountain inside her shirt and six days along a valley, and it was soft at the folds. Pike laid it on the oak and put two fingers on it, and read it to himself from the careful writing at the head to the foot, where five strokes stood side by side over a horned head and three wavy lines; and then he read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:227:"I'll not mark it tonight," said Pike. "That's not a thing I'd say of a fence. Take it in with you, and put it on your boards, and look at it with a lamp. If you bring it me in the morning I'll say it and write your strokes." He pushed the paper across the oak with one finger, until it lay in front of Cael. "And if you don't, it goes back where it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:3:It had been the afternoon of the hundred and nineteenth day when Karis sat down at the far fire with three clean leaves before her, and by the dusk of the hundred and twentieth she had risen from them only to sleep and to pace a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:5:She was making one leaf three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:13:She wrote small. At the head she set the three places by the names that carters gave them, since she had no better: the Fallow Ring, and the Long Stair, and the hall. Under each went its perimeter, which was sixty-two cords and twenty strides, three times over; and its radius, ten cords and a piece; and the number on its stake. Thirty-one. Thirty-two, on two bars. Thirty-three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:35:She had her own reach for each of the three, in cords. What she wanted beside it was the other man's: the figure that a surveyor had gone over twice with his pencil, in a room under a floor, until it cut the paper. She had heard it read out once. She had written it on her cuff, in the dark of that room, and the cuff had since been in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:71:And she had not chosen the third. She had laid that edge on two rings at a grey stone, in daylight, with five people looking, before any one of them had seen a reed. It had run off into a corner of the skin where a man once lifted his hand. They had walked along it for three days, in a strip a mile wide, and had come over a rise onto a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:It was entered. She sat and looked at what lay above it. There was a perimeter, three times, to the stride. There were forty-one kinds, and none strange. There was a line on a wall that was divided to a circle's radius, and stakes that were numbered in a row. And now there was a ruler lying through the three of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:155:They struck the road an hour from the marsh, and it rang under them. Every rut had a spine of ice in it. The pools on either hand had shut in the night, and the pale grass stood stiff and white to the knee, and their breath went up from eight mouths, counting the mules', and hung behind them along the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:193:"I owe the rest of you something. She's had it, as she set it, so it's free." He kept it short. "There's a place in me, under the breastbone, where nothing moves. All ten fragments go round it. I've read it three times, and it keeps. I don't know what it is, and I've been told to stop touching it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:35:Karis rolled the pen between two fingers, and Seln studied the shutter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:145:She went round the rest of them at her stride and a half, in the time that it took to fill a bucket. She turned Brom's hand to the light and gave it back to him. She laid two fingers on Karis's throat, and told her that she had her voice, and that she was not to lecture with it for a week. To Seln, who had not been hurt since he crossed the Line and seemed half ashamed of it, she gave the nod that she gave to a man of her own trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:251:On the gate-walk a lean man lifted two fingers from the stave of his own bow, an inch, and laid them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:177:It was Seln who turned the book over, and he did it with two fingers, as he would have handled another man's letter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:85:She did eleven of the rest with one hand each, and that took her less time than the knee had. There was a carter with a wrenched back and a woman whose tooth she looked at and would not touch. She laid two fingers on a wrist, or a palm on a forehead, for a count of ten, and told each of them the truth in a sentence. She put tape on a thumb. She told a big sullen man that there was nothing wrong with him that a week away from his brother would not cure, and the whole bench laughed, the man included.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:109:The healer did not answer at once. He saw her look at his right hand, where it hung, and then at her own wrist; and for the space of one breath there was the hunger in her face that he had seen at a ford and in a yard, when she had wanted to know a thing more than she wanted her supper. She put it by as he had seen her put it by before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:127:"She looked at your hand before she said it," Karis murmured. Her voice was pitched for his ear alone. "And then at her own wrist. I have seen that look on a person in front of a shut press. She was curious about you. Enter that somewhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:139:He gave her his hand across the sill, and she turned it over and gave it back. She put two fingers on Karis's throat. She gave Seln her short nod. She had gone round the five of them and found nothing that wanted her, and she sat down on her keg with her forearms on her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:155:She looked at the two fingers that were left standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:167:Across the pads of four fingers there was a cut, straight, as though it had been ruled. They had bled down into his cuff and dried. He had drawn that string three times with them, the last time when they were already open, and he had stood an hour in the frost with the stock in that hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:97:Lira's went up, and Brom's, and Karis's with the pencil in it. Seln lifted two fingers from his folded arms. Cael put up his own and took the paper off its tack, and Pike wrote five short strokes at its foot, side by side, and said the whole of it over to the grass with *taken* at the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Brom had loaded her by the well, in the grey, with the steading's woman holding the lantern. Two boxes hung from the pack-saddle on their own ropes, one to a side, and the third rode between them on a folded blanket with the empty pannier strapped over it. He had gone round the animal three times, pulling at each knot. Then he had stood back and looked at the load as he would have looked at a wheel that he had built himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:It was the hardest cold that they had yet walked in. The ruts of the valley road had frozen as the last grain cart left them, with every hoof-mark standing up like a thing cut in iron, and the pale grass on either hand was white to the knee. Their breath went ahead of them. On the gate-walk a lean man stood with his long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every morning that they had spent behind that fence; and when Lira looked up, he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had nothing in her right hand, and she lifted two of her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:47:He could do the second tonight. Four people were asleep or near it, and the fire was low. He had his fingers on his own wrist before he saw what he was about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:119:Three old men sat beyond her, at boards of their own. Cael had seen them under the ash for a hundred days without ever learning what they did, three grey heads over a pair of dice. The dice lay on the wood between them now. The eldest had his hand half closed over them, and he neither threw nor gathered them up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:171:"It is for what has been read and put back," said Karis, very low. "The girl at the next boards told me, on the ninetieth night. A captain takes a slip down, and reads it through, and hands it in again; and if it is a slip that weighs, he hangs it there, so that the next captain knows it has been weighed and by whom. There is hardly ever anything on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:193:The woman had laid two fingers on the shin, a hand's breadth below the knee, as lightly as on the string of an instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:281:She went to Karis last of the three, on her knees, and read her as she had read the leg, with two fingers on the inside of a thin wrist and two on the side of the neck. Her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:335:It was Brom who gave her one, the left, with its new seam. She closed two fingers on it, and her lips moved as far as three, and she let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:65:"So that's what there is," said Brom. He was sitting on the boards with his knees up, and he went through it on his fingers, as the healer went through a round. "Five that stop and one that doesn't. A floor that nobody can put a year to. A stake." He looked round the lantern. "And there's that clerk's paper. What do they know in the west, when it gets to wherever it's going?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:75:She had unrolled the calfskin on the boards, with its roads in double lines and its fords in three wavy strokes, and the thin paper lay on it with a ring of charcoal sitting on her pencilled one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:189:"And you've not gone back to it in eight days. I'm not asking you why, since I can see why from here. So you'll go back. You'll read it three times, on three different days, with a night between each. Read it frightened, and read it when you're not, and then you'll know what's there and what was only you." She buckled the strap. "After that you tell me, all of it at once and in order. You don't give me a piece of it now because I'm leaving and you'd like to have paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:33:Cael had seen that purse opened a good many times. It was a bag of soft hide with a drawn neck, and it wanted two hands: one to hold and one to count. Lira set the bottom of it against her hip and pinned it there with the heel of her palm. Her thumb and first finger worked the neck open, and two fingers went in; a coin came out between them and was laid on the step at the woman's feet, and the fingers went back for the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:217:"Good. Write it down before it changes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:325:It came home to his own two fingers, and he very nearly sat up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:327:He had not been sure that anything would go. He had sent it out from the palm on his breastbone a breath before, as blindly as a person drops a stone into a well to hear how deep the water lies. It had gone the whole length of him, in fits, and turned; and here it was, arriving under the two fingers that he had laid on the inside of his right wrist. It set out again without being told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:329:He knew nothing of how the thing was done except how it had been done to him, and so he had begun where she began, with two fingers and a palm, and the breath of a man asleep, and a count. At ten the short reading had given him the moving. It was his own gait, quick and slow, and no more. The rest he had from the piece of her that sat under his breastbone, which had felt the way of it for twenty minutes on a plank and had kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:357:He lay with two fingers on his wrist and counted what he could count. He knew where it lay, to the breadth of a finger. Nothing moved in it. And all ten went round it, and none through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:363:He was alone with it, as she had set it. He had seen to that before he began. He had waited until Brom's breathing went over into the long pull that meant an ox could have walked across him, and until the lantern had been dark for an hour. Only then had he lain down on the boards with his boots off, between a wall of barley and the ladder's head. There were four people asleep within six feet of him. For that one night the thing was nobody's but his, and he would have given a good deal to hand it to any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:129:The second time she had two fingers of her right hand on her own left wrist, and her lips moving. He saw them stop as her boot came down. She stood so for a breath, and came out, and they began again before her heel had left the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:287:He had no business at that gate. He kept the western one, and in three stays Cael had seen him leave his platform once, to watch six people go out by the east; and he was standing beside the east gate now, with his bow at the length of his arm, like a man who has been coming to the same place at the same hour for five days. He looked at nobody but Lira. He watched her come up the last fifty yards on the flat of her foot, with a strip of grey wool across her and a hand tucked under her collarbone, and he did not lift two fingers from the stave. He took off his cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:365:There were five people on it, under the window of the back room. They had seen a mule come up the valley and had come out and sat down, as eight had sat down at Oxhollow, and one of them was a man with his shin wrapped. She did not eat first. She went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist and her lips moving to ten, and at the end of the bench she turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:She was not sitting. She stood in the middle of the floor with her arms folded and her weight on her own boot, and her lips moved at each name. The five of them had the boards nearest the door, where they had sat on the forty-ninth night, and on the boards between Brom's elbows lay a sheet of cream paper with a lamp beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:25:"Which two have we gone over?" Lira had come back to the boards at last. "I can't tell from here. Half of them are trades."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:41:Pike drank. He looked at the fire, and then across the room at the lamp on the boards by the door, and then down at the leaf beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:111:They held the council once, at their own boards, with the lamp turned up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:181:The only sound was the fire. Cael looked at a cream sheet that had lain a month under a river stone, and saw it as he had not seen it at the plank: a thing with a ruled margin, lying on an inn's boards in a country that kept no paper. He had walked east for ninety days, and here was a piece of the west on his own table, with a seal on it that told him nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:39:*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:47:The last ferry crossed at noon on the hundred and thirtieth day, with two fingers of snow on its rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:71:An old man in Denvash had cut that leaf from his pad a long while ago, and had rolled a ruler in it for a boy who was going where there were no instruments. It had lain in a bag on the wrong side of a river for a month and more. And in that time the boy had walked off the end of another person's brass for three days and come to a marsh, and had stood at a fence of iron with a line going through it. He had not written one word of that west. The old man could not have known that there was a line. He had only known the boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:121:There was a thing that she had said at the marsh's edge, and she looked at that last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:141:She led the jenny in under the gate-walk. A lean man stood up there with a long bow at the length of his arm, and he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had never known him do that for her. She understood, crossing the yard, that it was not for her: it was for a girl with one arm, who had sat on those planks beside him and shot at a sack, and he was asking after her in the only way that he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:145:The two fingers lay down again on the wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:243:He had the strip from Denvash in his coat. It was cold from the back room, and it took the firelight all along one edge. He set it down on the skin beyond hers, with its end on the ring by the marsh, and slid it up until the two lay in one length; and he bent, and looked for daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:255:"I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:143:He did not say it kindly, and he did not say it unkindly. He turned his head toward the yard and lifted two fingers, and a boy came up the ladder as if he had been waiting at the foot of it, which he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:195:She had. She had come round the inside of that fence flat out, on two good feet, with the geese scattering, and had stopped dead three strides short of his ladder, with all that going in her and nowhere to put it; and he had lifted two fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:27:"There's none. I've no paper for it and I'm not sending to a plank for some." He scratched his jaw with the stick's end. "You walked my hill for seventy and brought me a no. I'd have paid the seventy three times over in carts, by next thaw, if you'd brought me a yes to please me. I can find a bed for three boxes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:35:He asked them in the yard, by the well, with the light gone grey. Brom looked at three boxes and then at his own left wrist, and Cael watched him weigh a road of six days against a rule of three, and put his hand up. Lira's was up already. Seln lifted two fingers from his folded arms. They had to wake Karis for hers, and she raised it before her eyes were properly open and asked afterward what she had voted for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:59:The first prints were a man's, of the middle size, set down evenly. Beside the right foot, here and there, was a small round dent, where the haft of a hand-axe had swung at his thigh and touched ground on the slope. The second walker had grounded a spear at every step. The third set of marks was shorter on the left side than on the right by the width of two fingers, every stride, and no deeper for it; whoever made them had been favouring that hip for so long that the body no longer argued. The fourth were the smallest feet of the four and went in the deepest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:99:Lira packed the right one. It took the whole of a spare shirt, torn in strips and wound round her taped foot until the foot was the size of the boot. When she stood up in it she was two fingers taller on that side than on the other. She walked a few steps, and stopped, and walked a few more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:129:The smith shrugged, and then sold Lira a long knife, which was the last thing the old purse ever bought. She chose it by its balance, across two fingers, as she would have chosen a lath; and when he told her that it was a skinning blade she said that it was whatever she said it was, and wore it home on her hip, outside her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:305:He thought about how to say it, for he had been on a great many boards. A crossroads in Ardenmere had carried him, in chalk, and a hall at Norhold, and four black ones in a sunk yard, and every one of them had told the people looking at it what to think of him before he opened his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:5:Cael took it out of Hesk's book, where it had lain since the ninetieth night with five strokes at its foot. Seln held the two sheets up to the fire, one in each hand, so that the flame stood behind both; and for some time he looked through them, as he had looked through the first on an inn's boards, and read no word of either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:23:The fire settled, and Cael kept his eyes on a sheet of cream paper and went back, since he could not help it, to an evening at an inn's boards twenty-two days before. Four of them had given reasons for the first paper. He had given his own last but one, and it had been two words, and he had been rather proud of how plain they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:47:He said it to the fire, in the mild voice in which he had once read out a floor schedule, and then he sat back out of the light. Cael understood him well enough: a broker's clerk had stood at a plank for a hundred days and watched which crew took which paper, and it had wanted only one fact about a boy to write the second sheet; the boy had supplied that fact himself, aloud, at an inn's boards, when he showed that he had never yet walked past a paper that paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:121:It was Cael's turn, and he had had the whole of the walk down from the inn to find a reason; he had found several, and he used none of them, since he had learned at an inn's boards what his reasons were worth when he dressed them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:337:The stars had turned as far as they turned in one watch, and he got up, without a sound, and went round the four sleepers, and the boxes, and the mule, as he had gone round every camp of theirs since the shelf. Then he bent and touched the girl's right shoulder with two fingers. He chose that shoulder with care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:51:Up on the platform the bowman stood with his bow at the length of his arm. He lifted two fingers from the stave as the cart went under him, and Lira raised the ash stick an inch from her knees, and set it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:61:Each of the five had a place on that river to look at. Brom looked at a slab in the near channel with a rim along one side, and as the cart drew level he put out two fingers and touched the tail-board, like a man greeting somebody across a street. Karis looked at a boulder the size of a loaf. Seln, walking behind, turned his head toward the deep water under the bar's flank and went on turning it until the place was behind him. Lira looked at the edge of the gravel, near the head, where round stones lay on round stones, and then she looked at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:"I ran thirty yards of gravel at that ford with nothing in my feet," said Lira. "No Wind. No burst. I caught a dog. I've never run like it. I didn't know I could, and I'm telling you it was the best thing I've ever done on my two legs, and it cost—" She stopped. Her hand had gone toward the stave, and she took it back. "It cost what it cost. And there isn't a person alive who can say whether it was good. There's a man with a bow who lifted two fingers. There's a book that says nobody died."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:33:He read it three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:123:There was one line more, which read *the shingle pair*; and at the next boards two men in tarred aprons watched it go on at the foot of the sheet. One of them looked across at Brom, and lifted his cup an inch, without rancour, as a carter does who has been passed on a hill by a lighter load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:349:Brom had started after her, and Cael put two fingers on his sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:435:He wrote the long entry on the sixty-ninth evening, at the boards by the inn door, where the light was. It was the hour at which he had once written that he was free, and he had not gone back to read that page, since there was no need.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:25:It was a room. He could see that much while he walked, and he took it in without stopping. It was twelve strides each way. The walls were a yard through, and they stood three times Brom's height at the far corner and were broken down to the height of a man's chest in two places on the left, with every break worn as round as a river stone. There was no roof and there never could have been a beam; the mist lay across the top like a lid. And the inner face of every wall was cut. The sunk lines ran in their courses from the water's skin to the broken tops, course over course, straight and hooked and crossing, nine deep where the wall was whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:215:The head lay at the ledge's lip beside his knee, half out of the water, quite still. He could have put his hand on it. It was the brown of the marsh, and smooth, and seamed down the middle where the two halves met; and it had no more face than the toe of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:89:In the row of chairs below the platform one of the four evaluators had stopped writing and was simply listening, with his pen held a little off the page, and Ilsev had not turned a leaf since Karis stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:93:She came half a step nearer the platform, and Cael did not think she knew that she had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:181:He said it to the platform, and then he turned and said the rest to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:197:The clerk turned a leaf, and there was a form pasted to the next one. Cael could see the edge of it from where he sat, a single sheet of registry stock under the seal with the river and the scale. He knew the hand that had written on its ruled line, though he had never seen the hand. He had seen the man; he had sat across a cold stove from him, four streets back from this room, and heard him say what he would write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:199:The clerk read the entry as he had read the others, in the code's own old words, as the form's column was headed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:201:"*Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:205:It was a small thing to see, and he did not think the benches saw it. The four district evaluators had been writing in a row, and at that line they stopped, one after another, like men on a road who see the one ahead halt. They were working officers. Every one of them had stood practitioners in frames for years, under a protocol that carried the Archmarshal's certificate as a matter of course, as a loaf carries a baker's mark; the certifying office had put its name to that protocol since before any of them was born. It had been asked twice for its name and had not given it, and the form had no line for why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. The refusal was the Archmarshal's; the form's old column had only Gault's title to put it under. Gault sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:215:It was enough, and that surprised him. He had wanted a wall, a month ago, and had been given a blank on a form. But he had watched Gault hand up a leaf because it was there, and he had heard Karis spend fifty-one minutes on a room that could not be moved, and he thought he was beginning to learn how the honest ones did it. They did not do it to win. They did the thing that was theirs to do, to its full weight, and let it be entered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:75:The wing posted station closures every week on a board behind the counter, because an enrollee could not be sent to a station for a frame reading on a day the station was shut. Nobody ever read the closures but the desk clerk, and the desk clerk only to answer a question. On the Fourth-day Seln read them, and there it was, in a district clerk's printed form: the river stations shut in turn for the Archmarshal's yearly inspection, which he walked himself, as he had walked it every year for a decade. The Ostrand station was down for the coming Seventh-day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:77:"He'll lie in Ostrand the night before," said Seln. "The inspection starts at the first bell. Officers of the Compact who are not of the seat lie at the inn on the wharf, the long low one where the seat's clerks eat in sitting weeks. They have done for as long as I have been in the trade."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:101:They went down on the Seventh-day by five ways, because it was a Seventh-day and the whole hill went down to Ostrand on a Seventh-day by any way it liked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:249:At the fourth station, a day upriver from Ostrand, the keeper had walked him through the frame room as keepers always did, proud of his brass, and at the end of it had opened a long drawer under the counter to show him the station's own sheets for the year. A keeper kept his sheets as a miller kept his tallies. They were the station's private record of what its frame had read on each child, before any clerk of entry had touched it, and this keeper wrote a good hand and wanted it seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:269:He had read forty margins on a scrubbed table in a borrowed room in Ostrand. Every one of them had had something in it before it was cleaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:271:He thought of the room in Ostrand, though he had told himself on the post road that he would not. Four young people and a man at a window. The man had stood a little to one side of the glass, where the frame hid him from the lane, the way the field service teaches its people to stand, and had copied forty orders in a round level hand that never once let itself be hurried. The Archmarshal had known what he was from the doorway. He had known, reading, roughly what the copying must have cost and how many years it had sat somewhere before it came to that table. He did not know the man's name, and he had been careful all the way home not to wonder it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:301:"Write what the instrument says," said the Archmarshal. "What you feel about it is information about you." He heard himself say it, the sentence every evaluator is told once, the sentence he had handed down to every junior he had ever had, and he heard for the first time what it would sound like to a man who knew what was on a leaf in a sleeve in a borrowed room in Ostrand. He did not take it back. It was the only safe thing he had to give the boy. "Keep her file as it is. Don't send for anything. If she does well, let her do well."
   185	Vastin did not move. His left hand lay on his knee, and once it opened and closed. His eyes were on the window, on the cupola, or past it, on nothing in particular, the way a man looks at the sky when he is listening for something a long way off. His face was quite still.
