editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/compiled-prompt.md:412:"That's the head of it, and I'll give you the rest in order, since I've had long enough to arrange it. You're going under a town to read. I've nothing to do down there. I can't read a stone; I'd not know a stratum from a stair. I mend what's put in front of me, and then I walk to the next. So you'll not be thinking that I'm coming, any of you, and I'll not have to watch you not ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/compiled-prompt.md:630:He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time, as he would have gone over a count. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on five sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.
