editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-20.md:33:The road found its tongue sooner than they did. A cart came out of a field gate ahead of them, piled with dung and steaming, and the man at the horse's head was whistling. He whistled the same four notes over and over, badly, and did not leave off when he saw six strangers under loads. He looked at the boxes, and at the beckets, and pulled his horse over to let the weight go by, as one carrier does for another. He had given Cael the same length of glance that he gave the hedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-20.md:213:He knew of two. One stood on the crown of the town ahead of him. It had been built to read whatever was brought near it, and he had written in pencil that he would not go within a square's width of the thing. The other sat underneath the same town, with a short strange name, and read how things were built. Whether a man who could tell how a wall stood could tell how a boy did, Cael had no idea.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/LEDGER-RULINGS.md:13:- **Names:** none minted (the clerk, keepers, porters, innkeeper and both holds unnamed). **Length:** about 35,200 against 34,000, accepted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-21.md:237:*Ran the reading on myself again. Same nothing at the center. I think the reading can't do it — you can't measure a stillness with a thing that only feels movement. I need an instrument for what doesn't move. Karis says there's a man in Treswick who reads how things are built. Maybe that's the instrument. Maybe I'm about to hand a stranger the one question I've never let anyone hold. Tomorrow. The walls are close enough now to have a color.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/pre-repair/chapter-24.md:25:Cael had. He had lain with a strange short word going round in him until the lantern was out, and then he had got up, and found a grey man sitting on a rung in the dark, as he had known that he would.
