editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/source-tracking.stdout.log:1716:    61	He noted the word. He did not do anything with it, because it was Karis's word for a man he had not met, and a word for a man is not an instrument; and because he could feel, at the edge of the noting, the shape of a thing he did not want to have arrived at yet — that he was going to walk into a city tomorrow and keep a promise about a station and hand the one question he had never let anyone hold to a stranger with a Path he had never seen, and that the reason he was going to do it was that he had just found out, against a post, that he could not answer the question himself, and never had been able to, and had spent six months mistaking the instrument's silence for the thing's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/source-tracking.stdout.log:1766:   111	*Ran the reading on myself again. Same nothing at the center. I think the reading can't do it — you can't measure a stillness with a thing that only feels movement. I need an instrument for what doesn't move. Karis says there's a man in Treswick who reads how things are built. Maybe that's the instrument. Maybe I'm about to hand a stranger the one question I've never let anyone hold. Tomorrow. The walls are close enough now to have a color.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/source-tracking.stdout.log:1817:    51	He walked them the way Karis would have paced a perimeter: the ring road round the bluff's foot from the bridge gate east along the river to the ferry landing and past it to where the first wall came down to the water and stopped, and back west along the wall's foot the other way, past the smith and the rope-walk and the brokers' yards to where the wall came down to the water on the far side. A circle, near enough. A line, with the city inside it and him outside, and everything on his side of the line a thing that had no rank and everything on the other side a thing that did, and the line was a wall, a real one, ochre, with a gate in it that a man with a coloured coat sat at, and he could not have crossed it if he had wanted to, and he did not want to, and the not-wanting was the strangest thing he had felt in a year.
