editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/source-tracking.stdout.log:3276:    87	There were strangers who came by right. He saw only one, a young man in travelling boots, who had the look of a courier. He took a folded paper from his coat without being asked, and the keeper with empty hands opened it and read it all, and read out a name and a district and a rank to his fellow; and the pen wrote for as long as it takes to say a line. The young man had his paper back and went up. He had been perfectly easy. He had been written in a book as a person is who has never had cause to mind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/source-tracking.stdout.log:3336:   147	There was an uproar. He went on before it was settled, since a stranger who stands and watches children is marked in any town; but he carried it with him up the quay, and it was a little while before he could have said whether it had made him want to laugh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-004/source-tracking.stdout.log:3452:   263	The word was strange to Cael. He had stood on floors against a good many Paths, and had the pieces of ten of them in him, and he had never met that one on any list of bouts; it was not a thing that a person fought with, then. He lay and turned it over, and it told him nothing.
