editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:89:What she felt instead was the thing she had not felt since she had walked out of Ternhall's gate nine days before its term. Across a floor, for the length of one breath, she had not been alone in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:87:The keeper is invisible. He had been invisible for fifteen years. The boy's people had never been invisible for a day. All year they had walked about the hill in the open and let themselves be counted, the girl on the walls with her chalk and the big one at the frames and the researcher with her lamp lit in the top window every night where the whole valley could see it. Five days ago, at the gate, the house itself had stood in the middle of its own court and read the paper out loud. Nobody on that side had hidden anything. They had simply been there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:175:Seln wrote his weekly return that night at the copying table, in the old pattern, as he had written one every week for fifteen years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:173:"I have gone fifteen years without leaving one of those on anything," said Seln. He was looking at the strip with real interest. "It was the second thing I was taught. I find that the hand does not want to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:"I've been a stop on my own route for nine days. I'm going back to being the route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:She had been empty for nine days. She had spent every point she owned on a mountain in eight seconds, and afterward she had not been able to strike a flint. But in all those nine days the sigil had been there. She had called it on the first night under the pale rock, and it had come, faithful as ever, and when she asked it for a point it had simply had nothing to give her. That had been a counter with a clerk behind it and an empty till.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:In fifteen years he had never seen anybody get anywhere that fast, at that size, in that direction, toward the danger and not away. And then the boy's arm had taken the return the way a stone quay takes a barge coming in too fast: it had not given, and it had not broken, and the force had gone on down through the boy into the boards. Two things, at the least, and the file's classification had a name for neither.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:19:His brief had categories, as every brief did. The second of them was the one the whole posting existed to fill, if it ever needed filling: *observed capability materially in excess of the subject's documented baseline.* Seln had filed under it seven times in fifteen years. Seven times he had been right. Seven people had gone, afterward, into files further up the coast that he had never been shown and had never asked to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:269:"I was not there to see," said Seln. "I imagine it was noticed. I left the keys where they could be counted." He turned his cup a quarter turn on his knee. "I have filed nothing today. There has not been a First-day like it in fifteen years, and I had expected to mind it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:277:He wrote four short lines, in the small square hand of a man who had filled other people's files for fifteen years, and read them over as he read anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:"Four, in the Compact's own digests, in all the years they go back, who carried this word. I've read their files, one by one, at the long table downstairs. The first was ordered out of the place they lived nine days after the Kindling that flagged them. The second in six. The third in eleven. The fourth in four." He kept his eyes on Yorlan's. "Not one of them had a hearing. Not one of them was asked anything that their file sets down. In every one, an order went out under an authority nobody looked at, because the word sat on the form in the place where a classification goes, and everybody who handled it trusted that somebody before them had checked. Three of those files don't end. They stop. I'm the fifth. I'm the first who's ever been let stand in a room like this and put the question out loud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:183:Cael knew the leaf, for it was the one with the noun on it, underlined once, and beneath that five counts and a distance, and she had carried it in her coat for nine days. It was a good deal fuller now than when he had last been shown it; she had ruled a line across under the *110*, and below the line her small exact hand went on to the foot of the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:55:It smelled of mules, and tar, and of something frying. A woman went between the tents with a board on her head and flat loaves stacked on the board; a man sat cross-legged in the grass with a harness across his knees, driving an awl through it with the heel of his hand. Three children and a yellow dog were trying to get a goat to go somewhere it had decided against. After nine days of ridges and a river, it seemed to Cael as loud as a city.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:197:A man who had been posted from town to town for fifteen years had been written into a book in every one of them, always by some other hand, and under a grade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:245:"Bronze," said Seln mildly. "Nobody has asked me in fifteen years. They had it on a roll."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:111:"I've a knife and fifteen years. It was never most of what I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:215:He stood at the long house door with an empty bowl on the sill beside him. She had a round of seven gates, and she was late at every one of them already, and nobody had asked this of her; and she had just spent five days of it on a road that every carter she had ever mended told her to keep off. He had wanted to say the sentence for nine days. He was glad now, with his whole heart, that he had left it for her, since from him it would have been a request, and from her it was a decision.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:129:"Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:181:"I know. I could feel it." She had turned back to the table and was already moving slips, and her face was pink. "Everything from the sixth to the eleventh is the renewals and the nine days. He conceded the renewals. He adopted my table; he said so again this morning. I'm proving a thing to a seat that the other side has signed." She took five slips out of the first row and laid them face down. "I liked those. She said they'd be in the part I liked best."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:13:"Iron Rank Three. Ember Path." Lira let that sit for as long as it deserved. "Sixteen years old. She walked out of a first-rank academy nine days before its autumn term began, and came here, and nobody can work out why. I've heard three reasons already and I'm not happy with any of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-09.md:125:The digest came on a grey morning nine days before Ternhall's autumn term, not a week after the reservoir. The name was on the third page, under a minor-tier academy she had to find on a map. Beside it, in the column for category, someone had written *observer*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:253:Nobody had sent him, and he was clear with himself about that, as he tried to be about most things. For fifteen years somebody had always sent him, and there had been a line on a sheet to say where, and a second line for the hour at which he was to be back. When he left the last of those houses he had expected that particular habit to be among the first to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:45:It was a bad hand. He was one of the miller's men, and a rope had taken it nine days before, against the drum of the hoist that lifted the sacks; it had been bound up in a meal-bag since, and it lay in her palms like a thing that had been trodden on. She had her eyes shut. Her lips were moving, and Cael found that he knew where she was by the count: at three she had the banks, and by seven she had gone along the bed to where it was stopped. He had lain under those hands for twenty minutes. He had a fragment in him of what she was doing, the tenth, and it sat quiet under his breastbone and did not stir toward her, since it stopped at the length of his own arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:197:He had been arguing about himself for a year: his enrollment and his file, the nine days of his renewal, his frame. And the whole while, on every north road in the Compact, on every morning the stations opened, this had been going on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:47:*Teaching assistant, Bronze grade, fifteen years' service, absent without notice from first bell of the First-day. Residence room found vacated; no personal effects; bed made. All working files completed and cross-indexed to date. Inner-door key, records-press key, post-room key and wing side-door key surrendered on the copying-table blotter. Floor schedule posted one week forward. No note left, no leave applied for, no address of forwarding held by this house. The house records no disciplinary matter, no irregularity of any kind in the two years of this posting, and no view as to cause. Query submitted to the placing district office per code; direction requested.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:The name was found in the drawer. It had nineteen postings against it, in fifteen years, and the last was a chartered house on a bluff above a river. From that house a return had come up every week for two years, on the same day, folded in the same way, with the subject's reference on the outside fold. The desk turned to the last of them, which was dated eight days before the query.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:77:They read fifteen years of it, and every line was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:83:That took them longer to see, for the returns were so full of true things, of hours and figures and the names of rooms. But when the readers had finished, they found that they held fifteen years of a man's eyes, and they could not have said what one of his sixty-three subjects was like, or what any of them had wanted, or feared, or carried. He had reported everything that could be checked, and he had left out all the rest, and he had done it so evenly, for so long, that it had been taken for a style.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:95:It was a mild day for a fire, and Seln had lit one anyway, as soon as the desk clerk's stool stood empty for the Seventh-day. There were nineteen bundles in the cupboard under the copying table, standing on their ends in the office's grey tape, each one his own duplicates from a single posting. He had carried them from town to town for fifteen years and had never been able to say what for. A man who copied his mother's ledgers at a copper a page did not learn afterward to keep only one of a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:169:He had spent fifteen years making true reports that told nothing. A second-year had just done it by accident, with a lamp, because she missed somebody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:203:On a Seventh-day he wrote a return. He had not written this week's; he had been at a stove. But the pattern was fifteen years deep and did not know that it had been retired, and while his hands laid sticks it composed the thing by itself, in the voice he kept for the audit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:247:Something lifted in him then, soft, behind the other eight. It came as it came at every dusk, in a meadow or on a road or at the turn of a carter's ruts. It was offering to take him out of the light. He stood ten cords and more inside the line, on the far side of a floor where a Shadow of fifteen years had found nothing to lift, and it came up in him exactly as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-22.md:275:The polite man at Torvin's door nine days ago, the one with the boots, had asked for *the circuit kid*, and had been given nothing. The man in the grey coat had asked about lodgers and letters and nothing else. Either they were two different men asking two different questions, or they were one man who had asked the same question two different ways. He did not know which. He could not think of a way to find out that would not cost a great deal more than four days at Torvin's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:149:"I know what his paper said the year he walked in. Copper, the same as mine." Pike dipped. "That's a good while back. There's no station nearer than nine days west, and he's never gone to one to be looked at again. By what I've seen him do I'd call him Bronze, if I'd to call him anything. He's not had it written. I've not asked him to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:237:"There was a slip," said Lira. "On the plank, by the left post, on a shingle. Did you see it? A chest to be carried to the turf fire. *Eleven marks.*" She put her finger on the ring with the bar through it and ran it off the edge of the hide, toward the door. "That's a road we know. We know both fords on it. We know where the bad water is, and which keeper counts hay twice. It's nine days and nobody on it but deer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:287:He had been turning one sentence over since a frame-house door, and he had not written it, because he had not been sure that it would stand. He had waited to see whether the country would take it back, and it had had forty-nine days in which to do so. He put the pencil to the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:113:Forty days. He turned that over next, because it was the only thing he had put into the room that evening and he was obliged to account for it. He had corrected the researcher because she was wrong, and a wrong figure written down in a room where he sat was, in some small part, his own wrong figure; that was the plain reason, and it had been his reason at the counter for fifteen years. But that had not been the whole of it. Forty days after the fourth sitting closed, a respondent who had not come back was entered as fled. Before the fortieth day, he was only absent. Between those two words there might be a place for a person to stand. Seln did not know yet whether there was. He had wanted the girl with the chalk to have the true figure in her head when she came to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:149:*For fifteen years I made the same decision every morning and never said it aloud, and it was quiet. The deciding has stopped being quiet.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:165:"You'll find her clock," Rooke had told Ephram. "Anybody can find her clock. That's not the question. The question's what you do with it once you've found it, because she's known it was there for fifteen years and she's still winning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:43:"From Denvash," he said. "Carrier's mark from the far end of the country, nine days on the road. It's been rained on twice. The inside should be dry; whoever packed it knew what he was about." He weighed it on his palm a moment, by habit. "A little under three pounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:241:"I resigned fifteen years ago. I just never filed it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:333:Seln sat with both hands folded on the satchel on his knees. He looked at the four hands, and then at the lamp in the window, turned up as high as it would go. He had been in a great many rooms, and had sat at the edge of every one of them for fifteen years, and he had counted five beans onto a gardener's table and called it arithmetic.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:"Every one of those twenty," said Lira, "we won knowing everything. That's how we've always done it, you and me. Everything on the table, every time, and then we go and use it on each other. There's been nothing better in either of our lives these two years. I mean that." She tightened the knot with her thumb. "And on the nineteenth it's worth nothing. Less than nothing. You know my leg, and since yesterday you know it worse. I know where your iron gives out. If we go on, we'll spend nine days guessing what the other one's guessing about what we're guessing, and we'll walk out onto those boards playing chess." She looked up at him. "Neither of us fights chess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:45:They lay that night in the keeper's cart-shed, under a heap of empty grain sacks that smelled of last year's barley, since their own blankets were at the bottom of a gorge. It was warmer than a bay of shale had been. He lay on his right side, where the ribs hung, and listened to four people breathing, and counted nine days to a fence in a valley where a healer meant to put her hands on him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:235:"She said nine days, and he said nine days back to her. I was there; I had my eyes shut, but I was there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:15:He turned that over in the dark, since he had the leisure. He had said once, at a fire in a meadow, that he kept waiting for someone to ask for his report. For fifteen years his reports had been asked for by a slot in a door; for a hundred days after that they had been asked for by nobody, and he had gone on walking the first hour of every road, alone, and saying what he found or not as it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:The seal was a stranger matter. It guarded the place where a boy had learned that craft, and the place was a man. Seln had spent fifteen years keeping other people's secrets in drawers; he had not looked to end by being one himself, kept shut on a hillside by a person of eighteen who had weighed a friend's throat against it and had still stopped to count who was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:53:He had been keeping off it since noon. It had stood at the back of everything that he did that day, as a figure stands at the back of a room: a square bar, driven at the far end of a measure, with a stamp on its neck that he had seen on every door he had walked through for fifteen years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:59:He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:255:"It is exactly what it says it is. There is nothing behind it at all." He said it in his mild voice, as he might have reported a floor schedule. "In my trade a man is taught four grades of pressure, in their order. The record. The instrument. The knock, which is paper nobody will own. And last the hand, which is a road with no paper at all. I was taught that the hand was the worst of them, and I believed it for fifteen years." He shook his head, very slightly. "I had the order wrong. What stood in the road was a glove." He looked down at the last line of the leaf. "*Given under the Compact's hand.* That is the only true sentence of its kind I have been shown this year. This is the hand. It does not have another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:185:"That's fifteen years of a clerk's pay," said Seln, to the fire, "in nineteen towns, by a man with a rented room and nobody to buy for. It is not remarkable. It adds up, if one waits." He did not look round. "I should prefer that nobody thanked me. I have been eating your bread for a week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:355:Cael looked at the two of them, and at a researcher with her pale fingers round a tin cup, and at a mild man in a dark coat who had sat at the edge of other people's tables for fifteen years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:15:He had worked rooms like this for fifteen years, in towns he no longer remembered the names of, and every one of them had a second floor that was not on any plan.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:23:He had spent fifteen years making sure of that, and he was good at it. It was, if he was honest, the thing he was best at in the world.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:95:He laid the four beside the compiler's question mark and looked at the two roads to one word. He did not know what the boy was managing, and he had never tried to know; fifteen years of not wondering past what was in front of him had kept him employed in rooms where wondering was not safe. Something was being held, and held well. He did not reach for more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:61:The ordinary silence after an advisory had a shape he knew as well as he knew the porter and his pigeons: an acknowledgment from the receiving office, a file marked closed somewhere below with a clerk's initials, a line in the weekly digest. He had read that silence ten thousand times. This was not it. The advisory was received. He knew it was received, because the dispatch slip in his coat said so. After that, nothing. No acknowledgment came back up the chain. The file did not reappear in the digest. The two letters on the fourth line of the routing slip were not on any other slip that crossed his table in the next nine days, and he looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:97:"I am saying it first, so that it is not taken for a vote on anybody else's arm. I have a cup of barley and a bowl in the middle box. In the cup are the kinds that I should swear to. In the bowl are the kinds that only a second floor can give me or take away, and I have had my thumb over the figure for nine days." Her voice was small and quite steady. "A man with apple trees told me that I should not tell a keeper from an eater in the one basket. I have one basket. I shall have one basket until I have stood on another of those floors with paper in my hand, and here is another, a day and a half from this fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:271:He had spent fifteen years as the first of those, and he knew what it was to write a true line at another person's bidding without being told what the line was for. He had written sixty-three subjects into bundles in that way, each of them a person with a face that he could have drawn; and he had sent every bundle up a stair to people whom he had never once seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:283:He had been taught four grades of pressure, in their order. There was the record, and then the instrument, and then the knock, which was paper that nobody would own. Last came the hand, which was a road with no paper at all. He had believed for fifteen years that the hand was the worst of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:325:He had lost an argument on a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second night past the Line. He had proposed that the watches should be his to arrange as he judged best, since he had fifteen years of judging them; and a girl with a ruled table had told him that the rota was not set aside for anybody's judgment, his own included. He had been angry for an hour and grateful ever since. A man who has decided things alone for fifteen years does not stop because he has resolved to; he stops when four people will not let him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:327:And there was the other reason, which he had given a boy in a loft. He had told him that he would sooner see a healer warned of what might be done to her than be used as the reason for keeping her ignorant. If that was good for a healer, it was good for a crew. He could not carry the danger off in the night and leave them to find a cold place under a tree. That was the service's way, and it had been his own, every week, for fifteen years: to know what was best for a subject, and arrange it, and say nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:261:He had been at the plank before the mist was off the river. The five had gone by it in the dark, two hours earlier, and he had heard the mule. Pike was setting out his book; and there was a shingle on the oak, six along from the left-hand post, which had hung there for nine days because no crew of any standing would trouble with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:167:Cael stood on the step and thought about it, and about the voice Seln had said it in, as if the sentence belonged to somebody else; and then about fifteen years of true reports going up a chain to an office that wanted something other than true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:165:"I filed true returns for fifteen years," he said, "knowing what they were for. Every word of every one of them was right. You get used to it." He moved one finger on the cloth. "You should not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:185:"Seln sat fifteen years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:273:"It is a good word for a report," said Seln. "I should have been glad of more words of that kind, in fifteen years." He considered the leaf. "There will be another name for this in the west, on the docket of a tin. I never read the docket. I do not know what they call it, and I shall not guess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:183:"The office," said Seln, "was once required to travel nine days by coach in the company of a clerk who whistled. The clerk whistled one tune. It had four bars. The office counted the repetitions on the seventh day as a matter of record, and stopped counting at nine hundred, and has never since been able to hear the tune without wishing to file a complaint." He picked up his cup. "That is all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:59:"It follows," said Jent, "that the second schedule to the petition is wrong in one particular. It suggests, at its ninth line, that the first renewal was sealed out of its order. It was not. It was sealed nine days after the panel sat, because an officer of an inspecting delegation held the registry's copy for his countersignature, as he was entitled to do, and the respondent's advocate has shown the seat the very leaf." He took one sheet from the folder after all, and held it out to the clerk. "The petitioner withdraws that line, and tenders the schedule again without it. I should not like the seat to rule upon a schedule with a mistake in it. Least of all one of mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:73:They had spent four days on that table of renewals, in the annex, at the start of the year. He had found the nine days himself, in his own old Log, and Karis had let him find them. And a man in a mended coat had just stood up before a hood and said that every line of it was right, and thrown away a piece of his own petition rather than leave an error standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:123:"We won three," he said, and made himself hear it as he said it. The nine days. Four days in the annex, and his own old Log, and her pencil stopping when he found it. "The table's in the minute now as the petitioner's own. Nobody can ever go behind it. Whatever they rule, they rule knowing every renewal was good."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:169:"Nobody leaves a registry kit. It's signed out. You don't get another." Seln's mild voice had an edge in it that Cael had heard only once before, by a grey stone, the night before. "I signed for a satchel and four keys in my first posting, and I answered for every one of them for fifteen years. A surveyor answers for this. If he had walked out of that door on his own feet, he would have carried it, or he would have come back for it. And the people who sent us here knew that it was lying. They wrote *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper. They left it where it was for as long as they wanted a thing in this country that somebody could be sent to fetch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:305:"You are setting your cord beside his, and his figure is in a registry book. I have spent fifteen years learning what that office's paper is worth. I should not care to see you rest a finding on a number of theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:31:He came along it at a clerk's pace, neither quick nor slow. He passed the turned post, where he had stood on every one of Cael's mornings in this hall for nine days, and he did not slow at it. He did not glance at it. Most of all, and this was the thing that went through Cael like cold water, he did not look down. Below him, alone in an empty hall at an hour when nothing was scheduled, sat the most stared-at student on the bluff. Every porter and lecturer and first-year who had ever crossed that gallery would have glanced down at him from pure habit, the way anybody glances at the one lit window in a dark street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:199:*For the record: he is better at this than I am. I've been at it nine days with a pencil and a lamp. He has been at it every hour he has spent on this hill, and for years before that, I'd guess, and in all that time I have found him put a foot wrong once, on a morning I built for the purpose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:223:"*Nothing to report.* I have written it every week for fifteen years. On the bottom of every return, in that hand." He did not look at the slip. "It is a true sentence. When there is nothing, that is what you write. It is the commonest line in the service."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:293:At the afternoon halt Cael took Hesk's travelling clock out of his kit and set it forward an hour, as he did in every tournament week. The city was still nine days off, and the clock had been wrong in the ordinary direction since he first owned it, but it seemed to him that the tournament's weeks had begun at that pole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-42.md:23:It was fair. That was what he felt first, before anything else. It was as fair as the grey book, which had a page on this man's grip and his rim and his slowness, because Cael had sat on a bench and watched him; and it was a great deal fairer than Cael had been, because Cael had never once walked up to anybody at a pump in the rain and told them. Keth had told him the night he won that somebody would start a page. It had been nine days. The somebody had started it at the beginning of the season, and had come to tell him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-42.md:55:"I didn't say it to be kind. I said it because he's had a season and you've had nine days." She went to the door with the bucket. "Go and write yourself down. I'll fetch the rest of the water. You'll only spill it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-42.md:271:"Your sort of thing," said Dace. "Something you've not done before." He turned to go. "I don't know who she is. I had the door ask her once. She wouldn't give a name. Smiled, and paid in good silver, and went and sat down. If I find out, you'll hear." He stopped. "And Cael. Bede asked for you nine days after Keth. That's quick. There'll be more like him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:The third sheet was the faint one, and it was the one that mattered. Seln knew precisely what eight feet of him was worth; nobody alive could have priced it better. He had spent fifteen years making himself a man no one could get near. Now, in the dullest hour of the day, he had drafted a lawful pass into his own range, made out to the single person on the bluff who could find him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:41:He had been keeping the count of days since the gate at Greyvane, every evening, in the margin of the observation notebook. It was five months and nine days, tonight, since a magistrate had stood up in a crowded hall and read him a ruling. He was fifteen years old. He had added the months up twice on the road against Hesk's last letter, because he no longer trusted any number that only he was keeping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:"There's a paper, priced so that we'd come. There's a floor at the end of it where a Wind can't run, and an Iron Skin is a big man, and an Ember can't light her own lamp, and a Shadow has a knife and fifteen years. There's a bolt, placed to bring a healer over a line. There's a fence behind her. There are four bows on a rim, far enough back that we can't reach them and near enough to shoot anybody who climbs. And there's a night." He drew a breath. "Every piece of it does one thing. It takes away what one of us can do. It's a list with six names on it and a line through each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:87:"Nine days he's been on this hill, and not a soul has played to him. Withrow stood still on purpose. Bracken's file stood up without anybody holding it. Rooke signed his sheets and went back to his cohort. Yesterday Gault ran three hours of true instruments and wrote down a flat line and stood up for it. The whole house has spent nine days being exactly what it is, as hard as it can." He spread his hands on the table. "So picture me walking in there and playing to him. Then I'm the only performer on the whole bluff, and I've spoilt the single thing this house has managed to let him see."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:243:*Shadow Path, Bronze, fifteen years' craft if he's a day. The Compact finally sent someone worth watching. So I'm watching.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:47:Karis had the marbled book open beside her bowl. When Lira sat down, Karis reached under her chair and brought up the two practice batons, still tied together in the middle with Lira's own hip bandage, in the ugly knot Lira had pulled tight nine days ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:63:For nine days they had not sparred, or talked about the bout, or watched each other cross a room. It had not been politeness. Without the silence, each of them would have spent nine days building a fight out of the other one's wound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:67:And he knew hers. If either of them had spent the last nine days thinking about the other's number, they would have walked out today to fight a ledger instead of a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:143:For nine days he had kept the read at its thinnest layer, to spare the hold. This afternoon, though, no one on the bluff would spare him a look. Every eye in the yard, the Archmarshal's among them, would be on the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:269:She knew his number, and he knew hers. And for nine days each of them had refused to build one single thought on the other's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:139:"No," she agreed, and nearly smiled. "It's an answer, though. I'm not giving you the number either, because you'll sit on the tier counting, and I'll feel you counting, and it'll be like having a clock in my hip." She shifted the cloth again. "The hip's fine. It's tight. It's been tight since the foundry. Tomorrow it's going to hurt, and the day after tomorrow it's going to hurt more, and then it's going to have nine days, and that's how it's going to be. Nobody's to talk about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:185:"I'm not thinking about the nineteenth," he said. "Not once. Not until I've beaten him. Every time I think about her, I think about how she moves, and then I'm in a bout with her in my head, and I'm losing to Merrick in real life while I do it." He set his heel again. "So I've decided. Merrick first. Merrick only. If I win, I'll think about her for nine days and not sleep. If I lose, it doesn't matter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:"My Kindling is in nine days." She said it as if it were part of the same sentence. "Nine days after tomorrow. I counted it from yours, not from mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:Nobody at the table needed the sum done twice. A square woman had stood in a gateway on the eighty-fourth day, with a mule's cheek-strap in her hand, and had said nine days. The road to the Fallow Ring went through Thornwater, and by no arrangement of theirs it went through on the day that she had named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:253:"The two holds," he said to Pike. "With our names. We'll be nine days on this, if it goes as it's written. If we're not down in their fortnight, don't keep a bull waiting on us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:99:She had been in her second year at a school in the south, Copper Rank Nine, one rank short of the threshold, and her instructor had told her in the autumn that she would cross before midwinter. In the winter her mother had fallen ill at home, three days' coach away. Gerda had gone to her instructor and asked for leave, and he had given it to her in the corridor outside the floor, nine days, *go*, and she had gone. In the nine days three of her recorded bouts had fallen due, and she had not been there to fight them. The leave had never been written down. Her instructor had meant to; she believed that still. Before he did, he had been offered a post at a larger academy in the north and taken it, and gone, within the week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:There would be nothing in it about a plank. That stood where she had put it, and a strip of bark was no place to move it from. There would be nothing about a still thing under a boy's breastbone; she had told him to leave it alone, and she would not be the one who put a finger on it from two days off. She would not ask after a mule. And she would not tell five people that she had been glad of their bowl, since she had eaten out of it standing, on purpose, for nine days, and they had every one of them seen her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:107:"North in the morning," Oryn said. It was not an answer to Karis; it was a fact about a route. "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor. I'll be on the road at first light and I'll be three days north by the time you're back at the board." She looked at him. "Thornwater's on my route. You know where that is now."
