# Simulated cold-read review — Movement 004

This is a **fresh-context simulated cold read**, not a real audience measurement. I read only the seven supplied manuscript files, Chapters 20–26, in their declared order. No editorial canon was supplied, so I can report internal coherence and apparent inconsistencies, but not verified canon violations. The movement reaches a deliberate closing hook and appears complete as a movement; however, the supplied manuscript is not textually complete because five visible `[[TIER-B: ... coordinator to supply protected text]]` placeholders remain.

## Unscored reader response

Interest caught immediately at “The hold came off at the first hedge.” The permission to speak after 281 days makes an ordinary hedge feel like a border crossing, and the silence that follows gives the journey weight before the town is explained. The next strong catch was Cael’s self-reading behind the hedge: the distinction between learning about the instrument and learning about the still place is lucid, consequential, and unsettling. The emotional center of the movement is Chapter 21, when Vastin gets himself down onto the bank before confessing why he refused the second reading. His physical choice makes the confession credible before the explanation begins.

Interest slipped slightly during the extended lower-town survey in Chapter 22 and again across the successive access explanations in Chapters 23–26. The rope-walk, two forges, two inn doors, porter boards, gate cards, three walls, side lane, stair, yard book, and lower door all reward attention, but the manuscript occasionally explains the same governing idea—names above, counts below—after the scene has already made it legible. The Tier-B placeholders are sharper interruptions: they replace the turn of Vastin’s confession, the discovery of the “hands” route, the Chapter 23 close, Karis’s first exchange with the historical practitioner, and the final clerk exchange with editorial notes.

Cael matters most. His fear is not merely that the station will expose him, but that the one person capable of looking correctly might look up from the evidence and see him; that is an intimate and specific threat. Lira becomes nearly as important through action rather than declaration: she sits outside the hedge, covers the window, walks between Cael and the square, and finally looks from the top without being claimed by it. Vastin supplies the movement’s strongest turn, moving from the man who classified others to a nameless “hand” who deliberately does not ask.

I expect the next event to be the dusty practitioner’s examination of Karis’s fifty-two sheets, followed by the harder question of whether he can or will read how Cael is built. I also expect the ancient dark construction under the registry to challenge the town’s account of its own foundations. Oryn’s delay makes her eventual return feel likely to arrive after Cael has already made a consequential choice.

I would continue. The close—“The tiers closed, and the dark opened”—pays off the movement’s descent through social and physical layers while opening a concrete mystery.

## Fluent thirteen-year-old lens

These scores are editorial judgments through a reader lens, not demographic findings.

| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
|---|---:|---|---|---|
| Opening pull | 8/10 | Chapter 20, opening through “We may talk” and Brom’s “Who says?” | The release from enforced road silence is immediate and concrete. “Line,” “hold,” “hound,” and “declaration” arrive quickly, so a new reader must infer several rules at once, but the human reaction keeps the passage moving. | High |
| Keep reading | 9/10 | Chapter 26, from the clerk writing “four” through “The tiers closed, and the dark opened.” | The movement delivers entry into the archive while withholding what lies below and what will be read. The local problem resolves cleanly and produces a larger one. | High |
| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 23, children playing gate: “Then I’m the gate”; Chapters 25–26, entry as “one, and four” | Making bureaucracy, social rank, and being counted into the machinery of a suspense sequence feels distinctive. The child’s answer compresses the theme without sounding like an adult lecture. | High |
| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | Chapter 20, Cael inventorying the ten currents; Chapters 23–26, the route from blue gate to buff gate to yard stair | Individual actions and images are clear, but the cold reader must retain many institutions, ranks, objects, routes, and rules. Repeated explanations help orientation but sometimes slow the forward motion. The placeholders create five outright gaps. | High |
| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 21, Vastin lowers himself onto the bank; Chapter 26, Lira shadows the four alleys | Vulnerability is enacted physically. Vastin gives up height and self-protection; Lira helps without demanding gratitude; Cael notices what those choices cost. | High |
| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Chapter 20, “You’re sitting on fifty-two sheets” / “Then they’ll be flat”; Chapter 22, the seventh bowl; Chapter 24, Brom making Karis eat | The humor belongs to distinct people and often doubles as care. It relieves pressure without dismissing fear or injury. | High |
| Action / suspense | 8/10 | Chapter 25, passage under the blue and buff gates; Chapter 26, Vastin’s stick slipping on step seventeen and the clerk almost asking | There is little conventional combat, but objectives, obstacles, exposure, adaptation, and cost remain trackable. The stair slip is brief and earned; the pen is a credible threat. | High |
| Progression payoff | 8/10 | Chapter 20, “He had laid a foot-rule against a smell”; Chapter 26, Lira’s “It’s a hill with a tax on it” | Cael gains a meaningful limit on his reading rather than a convenient new power, and Lira’s long pursuit of rank changes through experience. The payoffs alter their choices immediately. | High |
| Connection | 9/10 | Chapter 20’s square promise and seven bowls; Chapter 26’s lane beside the square and Oryn’s bowl under Brom’s lashing | Objects, injuries, promises, and relationship gestures carry across chapters and acquire new meaning. Oryn’s cutaways pause the town plot, but the bowl and healer debt keep her present. | Medium-high |
| Read-aloud quality | 6/10 | Chapter 21 bank confession; Chapter 23 close; Chapter 24 cook-house exchange; Chapter 26 clerk exchange | Most cadence is strong and dialogue attribution is clean. Several long sentences remain intelligible because their clauses are ordered. The five spoken-scene placeholders, however, make the supplied text impossible to read aloud as a finished movement. | High |

## Adult genre-reader lens

| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
|---|---:|---|---|---|
| Opening pull | 9/10 | Chapter 20, first hedge through “That is a town.” | The opening converts a rules explanation into relief, habit, and dry humor. It trusts the reader to infer the world from practiced behavior. | High |
| Keep reading | 9/10 | Chapter 24, the practitioner stopping one stride outside; Chapter 26, the iron door and final line | The movement layers two strong promises: the fifty-two sheets may revise history, and the same mode of reading may reach Cael’s still center. The archive entry is a satisfying threshold rather than a deferred non-event. | High |
| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 22, the paired forges and “They shoe by the tier”; Chapter 23, lawful entry by count; Chapter 26, the registry built above older foundations | Class structure is dramatized through labor, architecture, and paperwork instead of delivered as abstract politics. The administrative infiltration has the legibility of a heist without requiring deception. | High |
| Clarity / flow | 8/10 | Chapter 23, Seln’s report from the porter bench; Chapter 24, his dust map; Chapters 25–26, execution of that route | Spatial and procedural planting pays off accurately. The main deductions are clear on one pass, though repeated thematic interpretation and unfinished placeholders interrupt otherwise controlled flow. | High |
| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 21, “It is a place where two people can stand”; Chapter 24, Cael and Karis sharing the roll | Relationships advance through negotiated disclosure and useful acts. Cael’s distinction between friendship and shared ground is emotionally precise; supporting characters retain independent wants. | High |
| Humor / warmth | 9/10 | Chapter 21, “It is a curious thing, to be the slowest”; Chapter 24, “I am under several people’s terms today”; recurring seventh bowl | Dry wit grows from profession and affection. Warmth is carried by food, loads, silence, and who stands near whom, avoiding sentimental overstatement. | High |
| Action / suspense | 8/10 | Chapter 25, the keeper’s feather pausing over Vastin; Chapter 26, four alley sightlines, the stair descent, and the green book | The manuscript sustains suspense through surveillance and self-restraint. Repetition across several gates slightly reduces escalation, but each checkpoint tests a different vulnerability. | High |
| Progression payoff | 9/10 | Chapter 20, Cael reclassifies five “nothing” readings as instrument limits; Chapter 26, Lira rejects the ladder after seeing its labor system | Growth is epistemic, moral, and relational, and it produces behavior: Cael seeks a new reader without breaking the square promise; Lira chooses the load over her credential. Neither realization feels granted by narration alone. | High |
| Connection | 9/10 | Chapter 20’s posts and square prohibition; Chapter 21’s Vastin disclosure; Chapters 25–26’s entry as a hand | The movement repeatedly pays earlier plants in altered contexts: sitting, counting, carrying, looking down, keeping names, and reading from the bottom all converge at the archive door. | High |
| Read-aloud quality | 7/10 | Chapter 20, the ten-current inventory; Chapter 21, Vastin’s bank speech; Chapter 26, closing descent | The prose has deliberate breath, strong consonant texture, and reliable speaker control. Some syntactically extended paragraphs ask for careful voicing, but the principal deduction is for the five literal placeholders, not for the completed prose around them. | High |

## Internal consistency and boundaries

- **Canon:** N/A for verified canon checking. No project canon evidence was supplied. Nothing in the seven chapters alone establishes a canon violation.
- **Physical state:** Internally coherent. Vastin’s bound right leg, impaired left hand, stick use, reluctance to sit, need for leverage when rising, and difficulty descending twenty-two steps persist and affect choices. Brom’s right-foot trouble carries from the roadside stop into the climb. Oryn’s depletion after repairing the wrist directly constrains the childbirth intervention and delays her route.
- **Power limits:** Internally coherent. Cael’s ten currents retain distinct manifestations and costs. His two self-readings produce knowledge about the range of the instrument, not an unsupported diagnosis or power increase. Compression rises reflexively at the stair slip but is held because Vastin catches himself. Shadow continues to ask at dusk and is refused. Oryn’s work spends a finite reserve and leaves visible consequences.
- **Knowledge boundaries:** Internally coherent overall. Seln learns the town’s route through observation and porter testimony; Karis and the group know the practitioner’s name is written on his card even though he refuses to say it; and Cael withholds the source and method of his self-reading from Vastin. The first narrated use of “Cleon” at the end of Chapter 26 is therefore supportable, but because Cael is never shown reading or hearing the card’s name, its abrupt arrival can momentarily be mistaken for a viewpoint leak or for the still-anonymous yard clerk’s name.
- **Reserved disclosures:** N/A for plan or canon compliance. Within the manuscript, the ancient construction, the contents and ordering of the fifty-two sheets, the nature of Cael’s still center, and what the practitioner can read remain withheld rather than prematurely answered.
- **Chronology and carried state:** The main group advances from day 281 in Chapter 20 to day 284 in Chapters 25–26. Oryn’s parallel route is clearly separated and her lost day has a stated cause. The six-person main group, three boxes, Karis’s flat and hide, Vastin’s case, seventh bowl, gate-card corners, and count of four hands remain intelligible through the archive entry.

## Repair brief

1. **Location:** Chapter 21 after Vastin says he had never come away without a figure; Chapter 23 at the “hands” discovery and at the chapter close; Chapter 24 at the practitioner’s first response; Chapter 26 at the clerk’s final exchange. **Observed issue:** Five Tier-B coordinator placeholders occupy major dialogue turns. **Effect on the reader:** Emotional logic and procedural comprehension are interrupted exactly where scenes should turn, and the movement cannot be experienced or read aloud as finished prose. **Proposed scope:** Supply the protected text only, then check the sentence immediately before and after each insertion for pronoun, punctuation, and cadence joins; do not rewrite the surrounding scenes. **Strength to preserve:** The bank confession, lawful-entry discovery, cook-house recognition, and silent clerk decision are already well staged through bodies, objects, and withheld questions.

2. **Location:** Chapter 24, the practitioner says his name is on the card but nobody speaks it; Chapter 26, final archive-door paragraph, “Cleon pulled the iron to.” **Observed issue:** The proper name first enters narration at the instant the door closes, without a beat showing Cael reading it; the nearby yard clerk has also been emphasized as permanently unnamed. **Effect on the reader:** The name is available in principle, but its abrupt use can cause a momentary identity or viewpoint stumble at the climax. **Proposed scope:** Add a minimal earlier recognition of the name on the card, or identify him once as “the dusty man, Cleon” at the door; do not expand the exchange or make anyone say the name aloud. **Strength to preserve:** Keep his refusal to surrender a spoken name, the quiet book-like closing of the iron door, and the final “tiers/dark” cadence.

3. **Location:** Chapter 22 from the porter boards through the paired forges; Chapter 23 from Cael’s gate observation through Seln’s loft report; Chapters 25–26 across the blue gate, buff gate, yard clerk, and archive entry. **Observed issue:** The name-versus-count hierarchy is sometimes interpreted in narration or dialogue immediately after a concrete scene has already demonstrated it. **Effect on the reader:** The thematic accumulation is powerful, but a few duplicate glosses make the long approach feel more iterative than escalating, especially for the younger lens. **Proposed scope:** On a sentence-level pass, remove or compress only the second explanation when image and inference already agree; preserve every distinct obstacle, gate action, load, and character choice. **Strength to preserve:** The town’s class system is exceptionally tangible because it is embodied in shoes, eggs, water, cards, boards, backs, and pens rather than delivered as abstract worldbuilding.

